<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Gábor Hetyei
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Department of Mathematics and Statistics,
UNC Charlotte, Charlotte NC 28223-0001.
WWW: <span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">http://webpages.uncc.edu/ghetyei/.</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: August 24, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Combining a variant of the Farkas lemma with the Flow Decomposition
Theorem we show that the regions of any
deformation of a graphical arrangement may be bijectively labeled with a
set of weighted digraphs containing directed cycles of negative weight
only. Bounded regions correspond to strongly connected digraphs. The
study of the resulting labelings allows us to add the omitted details in
Stanley’s
proof on the injectivity of the Stanley-Pak labeling of the regions of
the extended Shi arrangement and to introduce a new labeling of the
regions in the <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan arrangement. We also point out that
Athanasiadis-Linusson labelings may be used to directly count regions
in a class of arrangements properly containing the extended Shi
arrangement and the Fuss-Catalan arrangement.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6>Farkas lemma, graphical arrangement, braid arrangement, Shi
arrangement, Linial arrangement, semi-acyclic tournament
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">1991 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>Primary 52C35; Secondary 05A10, 05A15, 11B68, 11B83
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Counting regions of a hyperplane arrangement is most often performed
by computing its characteristic polynomial and by using Zaslavsky’s
formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. This approach inspires
combinatorial labelings of the regions in several important
special cases: when all coefficients in all equations are integers, the
finite field method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Theorem 2.2]</cite> (explained
in detail by Stanley in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, Lecture 5]</cite> and in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, Section
3.11.4]</cite>) yields interesting models. For deformations of graphical
(or affinographic) arrangements, in which all equations are of the form
<math id="Sx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=c</annotation></semantics></math>, Whitney’s formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> and the gain graph
method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> open
a gateway to combinatorics. For certain deformations of the braid
arrangement, Gessel’s formula on the generating function of
labeled binary trees counted according to ascents and descents along
left or right edges (shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>) has specializations
that may be used to count the regions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A fundamentally different approach is to consider regions as sets defined
by inequalities. Three important examples of this approach are the
Stanley-Pak labeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, the Athanasiadis-Linusson
labeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> of the regions of the extended Shi
arrangement and the work of Hopkins and
Perkinson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> counting the regions in bigraphical
arrangements.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The main purpose of this paper is to show that combining a variant of the
Farkas lemma with the Flow Decomposition Theorem yields an automatic
way to encode the regions with weighted digraphs in any deformation of a
graphical arrangement in such a way that
regions correspond exactly to the ones in which the weight of the
directed cycles is negative. This approach generalizes the
representation of the regions of the Linial arrangement with semiacyclic
tournaments and associates bounded regions to strongly connected
digraphs. Using this approach one may easily fill in the omitted details
in Stanley’s proof of the injectivity of the Stanley-Pak labeling in the
extended Shi arrangement, provide an alternative proof of Stanley’s formula for
exponential arrangements, and find a new labeling of the regions of the
<math id="Sx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan arrangement. This approach also generalizes the partial
orientations introduced by Hopkins and
Perkinson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to label the regions of a
bigraphical arrangement (they used the same variant of the Farkas lemma)
and naturally explains why sleek posets
represent the regions of the Linial arrangement and why bounded regions
of an interval order arrangement are in bijection with posets whose
incomparability graph is connected. In an effort to keep the paper’s
size manageable, focus is on deformations of the braid arrangement, but
the key ideas apply to the deformations of all graphical arrangements.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is structured as follows. Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> contains
the key bijection between regions of a
graphical arrangement and weighted digraphs. Section <a href="#S3" title="3. The poset of gains and sparse deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> treats
deformations of the braid
arrangement which are <span id="Sx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sparse</span> in the sense that at most two
hyperplanes are associated to each edge. The Linial arrangement and
interval order arrangement are important examples, but the bigraphical
arrangements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> are also related: they are also
sparse in the above sense, however their underlying graph can be any
simple graph, not only the complete graph. A key new idea idea is the
notion of <span id="Sx1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gains</span>,
using the fact that banning nonnegative directed
cycles is equivalent to restricting our attention to weighted digraphs,
in which negative weights represent costs, and the achievable maximum
gain along any walk is finite. This
idea is also used in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Separated deformations and the weak triangle inequality ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> which treats
deformations of the braid arrangement which contain the hyperplane
<math id="Sx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for each pair <math id="Sx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}</annotation></semantics></math>: their regions refine the
regions of the braid arrangement. Due to the
presence of nonnegative weights, Dijkstra’s algorithm can not be used, but
for an important special case, when the
weight function satisfies the <span id="Sx1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weak triangle inequality</span>, the gain
function may be computed by building a tree recursively. This gain
function is used in the final Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> to introduce a
bijective labeling of the regions of an extended Shi arrangement with labeled
<math id="Sx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan paths. It is worth exploring in the future whether some
variant of the gain function could play a role similar to parking
functions in a broader setting. Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> focuses on
integral deformations of the braid arrangement, in which the constants
<math id="Sx1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in the equations
<math id="Sx1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=c</annotation></semantics></math> form a contiguous interval of integers. Among other results
this section contains limits on the sizes of the directed cycles which
we need to check to verify that a weighted digraph represents a nonempty
region.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The last two sections contain applications of our approach to the
extended Shi and <math id="Sx1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan arrangements, and also extend the use of
Athanasiadis-Linusson diagrams to a broader class of arrangements. It turns
out that this approach provides the fastest way to count the regions in the
<math id="Sx1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan arrangement, and also in a class of hyperplane arrangements
properly containing the extended Shi arrangements.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The highlighted variant of the Farkas lemma is likely suitable to count
regions of a hyperplane arrangement in many other settings as well.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Two classical results</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following variant of the Farkas lemma has also been used
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. It is originally due to
Carver <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Theorem 3]</cite>, who stated it in a slightly different
form. The formulation below may be found
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Carver)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a real <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrix and let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> be a real <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times 1</annotation></semantics></math>
column vector. Then the system of inequalities <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ax&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ax&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math> has no solution if
and only if there is a nonzero real <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times 1</annotation></semantics></math> row vector <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yA=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yA=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yb\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yb\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The other classical result we need is the Flow Decomposition
Theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Theorem 8.8]</cite>, originally due to
Gallai <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. We apply it in the
following setting. Consider a directed graph with edge set <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. A <span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">circulation</span> is a function <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:E\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:E\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
<math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v=t(e)}f(e)=\sum_{v=h(e)}f(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v=t(e)}f(e)=\sum_{v=h(e)}f(e)</annotation></semantics></math>
at every vertex <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(e)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(e)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the tail, respectively the head
of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. We set no capacity constraint (upper bound) on the values
<math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(e)</annotation></semantics></math>. The simplest example of a nonzero circulation is supported by a <span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">directed cycle</span> <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(e_{i})=t(e_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(e_{i})=t(e_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> holds for
<math id="S1.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,k-1</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(e_{k})=t(e_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(e_{k})=t(e_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. We identify the
directed cycle <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>
with the circulation <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> that assigns <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to the edges
<math id="S1.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and zero to all other edges. The restriction of the
Flow Decomposition Theorem to circulations is the following
statement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every not identically zero circulation <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as a positive
linear combination of directed cycles. Moreover, a directed edge <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>
appears in at least one of these cycles if and only if <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(e)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(e)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will apply Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.1. Two classical results ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> to digraphs with <span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a cost
function</span> assigning a (possibly negative) cost <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(e)</annotation></semantics></math> to each directed
edge <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>, the cost of a circulation <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is the sum <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(f)=\sum_{e\in E}c(e)\cdot f(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(f)=\sum_{e\in E}c(e)\cdot f(e)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The everywhere zero flow is the unique minimum cost circulation if and
only if the cost of every directed cycle is strictly positive.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, if there is a directed cycle whose cost is zero or negative,
then either the cost of the everywhere zero flow is not minimal or it is
not the only circulation whose cost is zero. On the other hand, if all
directed cycles have a positive cost then every nonzero circulation has
a positive cost by Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1.1. Two classical results ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Deformations of a graphical arrangement</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A hyperplane arrangement <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite collection of
hyperplanes in a <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional real vector space, which partition
the space into regions. We may use the poset
<math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{{\mathcal{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{{\mathcal{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of nonempty intersections (ordered by reverse
inclusion) of the hyperplanes to count the regions. The <span id="S1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">characteristic polynomial</span> <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi({\mathcal{A}},q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi({\mathcal{A}},q)</annotation></semantics></math> of the arrangement
is defined as</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi({\mathcal{A}},q)=\sum_{x\in L_{{\mathcal{A}}}}\mu(\widehat{0},x)q^{\dim(x)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi({\mathcal{A}},q)=\sum_{x\in L_{{\mathcal{A}}}}\mu(\widehat{0},x)q^{\dim(x)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Möbius function of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{{\mathcal{A}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{{\mathcal{A}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the entire vector space.
The numbers <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r({\mathcal{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r({\mathcal{A}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b({\mathcal{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b({\mathcal{A}})</annotation></semantics></math> of all, respectively
bounded regions may be found using
<span id="S1.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Zaslavsky’s formulas</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, stating</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r({\mathcal{A}})=(-1)^{d}\chi({\mathcal{A}},-1)\quad\mbox{and}\quad b({\mathcal{A}})=(-1)^{\operatorname{rk}(L_{{\mathcal{A}}})}\chi({\mathcal{A}},1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r({\mathcal{A}})=(-1)^{d}\chi({\mathcal{A}},-1)\quad\mbox{and}\quad b({\mathcal{A}})=(-1)^{\operatorname{rk}(L_{{\mathcal{A}}})}\chi({\mathcal{A}},1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Various methods are known to compute the characteristic polynomial.
In the case when all equations have only integer coefficients, the
characteristic polynomial may be computed using the finite field
method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Theorem 2.2]</cite>. When <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is
constructed from a graph, Whitney’s formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> or the gain graph
method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> may be used.
We focus on <span id="S1.SS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deformations of the braid
arrangement</span>, and our main reference is the work of
Postnikov and Stanley <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. The <span id="S1.SS2.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">braid
arrangement</span> or <span id="S1.SS2.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Coxeter
arrangement of type <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the collection of hyperplanes</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=0,\quad 1\leq i&lt;j\leq n" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=0,\quad 1\leq i&lt;j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">in the subspace <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, given by
<math id="S1.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}+x_{2}+\cdots+x_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}+x_{2}+\cdots+x_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The braid arrangement is also a special case of
a <span id="S1.SS2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">graphical arrangement <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math></span> induced by a simple
connected undirected graph <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with edge set <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(G)</annotation></semantics></math> on the vertex set
<math id="S1.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. It consists of the hyperplanes</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=0,\quad\{i,j\}\in E(G)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=0,\quad\{i,j\}\in E(G)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">in <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the braid arrangement is <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}_{K_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}_{K_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S1.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the complete graph on <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. A <span id="S1.SS2.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deformation</span> of a
graphical arrangement consists of replacing each hyperplane <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=0</annotation></semantics></math>
with a set of hyperplanes</p>
<table id="S1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=a_{ij}^{(1)},a_{ij}^{(2)},\ldots,a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})},\quad\{i,j\}\in E(G)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=a_{ij}^{(1)},a_{ij}^{(2)},\ldots,a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})},\quad\{i,j\}\in E(G),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> are nonnegative integers and the <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are real
numbers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Our choice to restrict our definition of a graphical arrangement to
connected graphs only and restrict our hyperplanes to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
consistent generalization of the notation and terminology
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Another option is to consider
graphical arrangements in the entire space <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, associate
graphical arrangements to disconnected undirected graphs as well, but
consider relative bounded regions instead. We refer the
interested reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Definition 1.7]</cite> for
the exact definitions of that approach. See
also Remark <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Remark 2.2. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Well-studied deformations of the braid
arrangement are the <span id="S1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">truncated affine arrangements
<math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,b}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,b}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The integer parameters <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy
<math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+b\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+b\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and the hyperplanes are</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=1-a,2-a,\ldots,b-1\quad 1\leq i&lt;j\leq n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=1-a,2-a,\ldots,b-1\quad 1\leq i&lt;j\leq n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{0,2}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{0,2}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S1.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Linial arrangement</span>,
<math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{1,2}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{1,2}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S1.SS2.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Shi arrangement</span>
<math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,a+1}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,a+1}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S1.SS2.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">extended Shi
arrangement</span>, <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{2,2}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{2,2}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S1.SS2.p2.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Catalan arrangement</span>,
and <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.SS2.p2.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-Catalan
arrangement</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the study of truncated affine arrangements, without loss of
generality we may assume that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq b</annotation></semantics></math> holds:
replacing each vector <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{n},x_{n-1},\ldots,x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{n},x_{n-1},\ldots,x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>
sends the arrangement <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{ab}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{ab}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> into the arrangement
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{ba}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{ba}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, since the equation corresponding to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=c</annotation></semantics></math>
is the equation <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n+1-j}-x_{n+1-i}=-c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n+1-j}-x_{n+1-i}=-c</annotation></semantics></math> after the linear
transformation.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Describing a region in a deformation of a graphical arrangement amounts to
determining whether a system of linear inequalities of the form</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;M_{ij},\quad 1\leq i&lt;j\leq n" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;M_{ij},\quad 1\leq i&lt;j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">has a solution in <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we assume that <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}&lt;M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}&lt;M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all
<math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> and we allow <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}=-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}=-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>
respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We call the solution set of a system of linear inequalities of the form
(<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) in the set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> a weighted digraphical polytope.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When we count regions without regard to their
boundedness, we may equivalently consider the solution set of
(<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here the solution set is either empty
or unbounded: for any real number <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>,
replacing each <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}+r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}+r</annotation></semantics></math> leaves all differences <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>
unchanged. On the other hand, if we subtract <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from each
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a point in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">To each system of inequalities (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) we create its associated weighted digraph as follows. For each <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, if
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}&gt;-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}&gt;-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, we create
directed edge <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> with weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>
we also create a directed edge <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> with weight
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. An <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle in the associated weighted
digraph is a directed cycle, along which the sum of the labels is
nonnegative. We call the associated weighted digraph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic,
if it contains no <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The associated weighted digraph uniquely encodes the
system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) (but not its solution set). Our definition of an
<math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle is designed
to match the conventions of labeling the regions of the
Linial arrangement using semiacyclic tournaments, see
Example <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Example 2.9. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a> below. To maintain this compatibility we make the
following definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cost of an edge in an associated weighted digraph
of a system of inequalities (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) is the negative of its weight.
Equivalently, the weight is the amount we gain by using an edge.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Hence an <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle is a cycle with non-positive cost. Using
Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 1.1 (Carver). ‣ 1.1. Two classical results ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> we obtain the following characterization of
nonempty weighted digraphical polytopes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A weighted digraphical polytope given by a system of inequalities of the form
(<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) is not empty if and only if the weighted digraph
associated to (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">When we rewrite the inequalities (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) in the form <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ax&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ax&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>, we
must rewrite all inequalities <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty\neq m_{ij}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty\neq m_{ij}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}-x_{i}&lt;-m_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}-x_{i}&lt;-m_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us associate to each such inequality a distinct
variable <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. We keep all remaining inequalities of the form
<math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;M_{ij}\neq\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;M_{ij}\neq\infty</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged, and we associate to each such
inequality a distinct variable <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 1.1 (Carver). ‣ 1.1. Two classical results ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> the system of
inequalities (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) has no solution if an only if there is a pair
of vectors <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u=(u_{ij}\&gt;:\&gt;1\leq i&lt;j\leq n,m_{ij}\neq-\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">≠</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=(u_{ij}\&gt;:\&gt;1\leq i&lt;j\leq n,m_{ij}\neq-\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="v=(v_{ij}\&gt;:\&gt;1\leq i&lt;j\leq n,M_{ij}\neq\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=(v_{ij}\&gt;:\&gt;1\leq i&lt;j\leq n,M_{ij}\neq\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>,
such that the following are satisfied:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All coordinates <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> are nonnegative and at
least one of the vectors <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is not zero.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\sum_{j&gt;i}u_{ij}+\sum_{k&lt;i}u_{k,i}-\sum_{k&lt;i}v_{k,i}+\sum_{j&gt;i}v_{ij}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\sum_{j&gt;i}u_{ij}+\sum_{k&lt;i}u_{k,i}-\sum_{k&lt;i}v_{k,i}+\sum_{j&gt;i}v_{ij}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The inequality</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\sum_{i&lt;j}u_{ij}\cdot m_{ij}+\sum_{i&lt;j}v_{ij}\cdot M_{ij}\leq 0\quad\mbox{holds}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>holds</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\sum_{i&lt;j}u_{ij}\cdot m_{ij}+\sum_{i&lt;j}v_{ij}\cdot M_{ij}\leq 0\quad\mbox{holds}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Condition (2) amounts to stating the following: if for
each <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}\neq-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}\neq-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> we let <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{ij}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{ij}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>
units flow from <math id="S2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and for each <math id="S2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}\neq\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}\neq\infty</annotation></semantics></math>
we let <math id="S2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{ij}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{ij}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> units flow from <math id="S2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a
circulation. Let us call a circulation <math id="S2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S2.p4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-ascending</span><span id="S2.p4.2.3" class="ltx_text"> if it satisfies (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In item 3 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>). If we think of the
numbers <math id="S2.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-m_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-m_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> as costs, then an <math id="S2.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending
circulation is simply a circulation whose cost is not positive.
By Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 1.3. ‣ 1.1. Two classical results ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> there is a nonzero <math id="S2.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending circulation
if and only if there is also and <math id="S2.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The net flow between <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> does not
change if we decrease both <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> by the same
positive real number <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the sum on the left hand side of
(<a href="#S2.E2" title="In item 3 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>) decreases by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}-m_{ij}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}-m_{ij}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence if there
is an <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending circulation, then there is also such a
circulation in which for any <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> at most one of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is
positive. A related observation is that there is an <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending
cycle of length <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}-m_{ij}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}-m_{ij}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> holds for some
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>: in this case <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> has no solution.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We may also introduce an edge
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math>) of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>
whenever <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i,j}=-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i,j}=-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i,j}=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i,j}=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>)
holds, and we may extend the addition operation to <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}\cup\{-\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}\cup\{-\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> by setting <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+(-\infty)=(-\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+(-\infty)=(-\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>
for any <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in{\mathbb{R}}\cup\{-\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in{\mathbb{R}}\cup\{-\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This addition makes no
substantial difference as no <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle could contain a
directed edge of weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 2.5. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> may be rephrased as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the associated weighted digraph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> encoding a system of
inequalities of the form (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) and think of the weight <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(e)</annotation></semantics></math> as money
we gain when we walk from the tail of the edge <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> to its head. Then
the system of inequalities (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) has a nonempty solution set if
and only if we lose money along any closed walk.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraphs are exactly the ones to
which the Floyd-Warshall algorithm may be applied to find a unique
minimum cost directed path between any pair of vertices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Each region of the Linial arrangement <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{0,2}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{0,2}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
is described by a set of inequalities</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;M_{ij},\quad 1\leq i&lt;j\leq n" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;M_{ij},\quad 1\leq i&lt;j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where each of these inequalities is either <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. The associated weighted digraph is a tournament: for each pair <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}</annotation></semantics></math>, exactly one of the directed
edges <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the digraph. This
tournament contains no <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle if and only if it is semiacyclic, as defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. The observation
that semiacyclic tournaments are in bijection with the regions of the
Linial arrangement was independently made by Postnikov and Stanley and
by Shmulik Ravid.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our next result helps identify the bounded regions in a deformation of
the braid arrangement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A weighted digraphical polytope, given by a system of inequalities of the form
(<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>), is not empty and bounded if and only if the associated
weighted digraph is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic and it is strongly connected.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 2.5. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> we may assume that the associated weighted
digraph is <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic: this is equivalent to assuming
that our polytope is not the empty set.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume first that the associated weighted digraph is not strongly
connected. Then the set <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> may be partitioned into two
disjoint subsets <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for each <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}\in V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}\in V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}\in V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}\in V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the directed edge <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}\leftarrow v_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}\leftarrow v_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> does not belong to
the associated weighted digraph. Let <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be any point
satisfying (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). We claim that the point <math id="S2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,x_{n}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1}^{\prime},\ldots,x_{n}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
given by</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{v}^{\prime}=\begin{cases}x_{v}+\frac{t}{|V_{1}|}&amp;\mbox{if $v\in V_{1}$}\\
x_{v}-\frac{t}{|V_{2}|}&amp;\mbox{if $v\in V_{2}$}\\
\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{v}^{\prime}=\begin{cases}x_{v}+\frac{t}{|V_{1}|}&amp;\mbox{if $v\in V_{1}$}\\
x_{v}-\frac{t}{|V_{2}|}&amp;\mbox{if $v\in V_{2}$}\\
\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">also satisfies (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) for all <math id="S2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed <math id="S2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{\prime}-x_{j}^{\prime}=x_{i}-x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{\prime}-x_{j}^{\prime}=x_{i}-x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>
holds if both <math id="S2.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> belong to <math id="S2.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or both of them belong to
<math id="S2.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We are left to consider the inequalities where one of the indices
belongs to <math id="S2.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the other to <math id="S2.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Without loss of generality we
may assume <math id="S2.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. If there is any bound on
<math id="S2.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is of the form <math id="S2.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> and it is of the
form <math id="S2.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}-x_{i}&lt;M_{j,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}-x_{i}&lt;M_{j,i}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math>. In either case, the inequality is even
more valid if we increase <math id="S2.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and we decrease <math id="S2.p9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S2.p9.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Observe finally that we increased <math id="S2.p9.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates by
<math id="S2.p9.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t/|V_{1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t/|V_{1}|</annotation></semantics></math> and we decreased <math id="S2.p9.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V_{2}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V_{2}|</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates by
<math id="S2.p9.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t/|V_{2}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t/|V_{2}|</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S2.p9.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{\prime}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{\prime}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The value of <math id="S2.p9.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is not bounded from above, hence our
digraphical polytope must be unbounded.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume next that the associated weighted digraph <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> defining a weighted
digraphical polytope <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\subset V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\subset V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly
connected. We show by induction on <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> that <math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded. There is
nothing to prove for <math id="S2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a single point and every
directed graph with a single vertex is strongly connected. For <math id="S2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math>,
the region defined by <math id="S2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1,2}&lt;x_{1}-x_{2}&lt;M_{1,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1,2}&lt;x_{1}-x_{2}&lt;M_{1,2}</annotation></semantics></math> is an open interval, and
it is bounded exactly when both <math id="S2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1,2}\neq-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1,2}\neq-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1,2}\neq\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1,2}\neq\infty</annotation></semantics></math> hold, which is precisely the case when the vertices <math id="S2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>
are linked by directed edges both ways. Assume from now on that <math id="S2.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>
holds. Since the digraph is strongly connected, neither the set
<math id="S2.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{In}(n)=\{i\&gt;:\&gt;i\rightarrow n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>In</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.498em" rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{In}(n)=\{i\&gt;:\&gt;i\rightarrow n\}</annotation></semantics></math> nor the set
<math id="S2.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Out}(n)=\{j\&gt;:\&gt;n\rightarrow j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Out</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.498em" rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Out}(n)=\{j\&gt;:\&gt;n\rightarrow j\}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty. Each weighted
arrow <math id="S2.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow n</annotation></semantics></math> represents an inequality <math id="S2.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i,n}&lt;x_{i}-x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i,n}&lt;x_{i}-x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> which
we rearrange as <math id="S2.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n}&lt;x_{i}-m_{i,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n}&lt;x_{i}-m_{i,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each weighted directed edge <math id="S2.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math>
represents an inequality <math id="S2.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}-x_{n}&lt;M_{j,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}-x_{n}&lt;M_{j,n}</annotation></semantics></math> which we rearrange as
<math id="S2.p10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-x_{n}&lt;M_{j,n}-x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-x_{n}&lt;M_{j,n}-x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each pair <math id="S2.p10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S2.p10.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\operatorname{In}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>In</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\operatorname{In}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\operatorname{Out}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Out</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\operatorname{Out}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, we take the
sum of the inequalities represented by <math id="S2.p10.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leftarrow n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leftarrow n</annotation></semantics></math> and obtain <math id="S2.p10.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;x_{i}-m_{i,n}+M_{j,n}-x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;x_{i}-m_{i,n}+M_{j,n}-x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. We rearrange this inequality
as <math id="S2.p10.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i,n}-M_{j,n}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i,n}-M_{j,n}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p10.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> and as <math id="S2.p10.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}-x_{i}&lt;-m_{i,n}+M_{j,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}-x_{i}&lt;-m_{i,n}+M_{j,n}</annotation></semantics></math>
if <math id="S2.p10.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>. We add these inequalities, remove the inequalities
involving <math id="S2.p10.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we obtain the definition of a weighted digraphical
polytope <math id="S2.p10.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}\subset V_{n-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}\subset V_{n-2}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Note that we may have created several
upper and lower bounds for the same <math id="S2.p10.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> but we only need to
consider the greatest lower bound and the least upper bound.) Its
associated weighted digraph <math id="S2.p10.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is still strongly connected: if a
directed path, connecting two elements of the set <math id="S2.p10.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>
does not pass through <math id="S2.p10.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> then the same walk is also present in <math id="S2.p10.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, if
it passes through <math id="S2.p10.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we may replace the subwalk <math id="S2.p10.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow n\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow n\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p10.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with a single edge <math id="S2.p10.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p10.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as we
created the edge <math id="S2.p10.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> when we took the sum of the
inequalities associated to <math id="S2.p10.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math>. By the
induction hypothesis <math id="S2.p10.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a bounded (or empty) polytope. Since <math id="S2.p10.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
is bounded, there is a cube <math id="S2.p10.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-R,R]^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-R,R]^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> containing it for
some <math id="S2.p10.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider now
any point <math id="S2.p10.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n})\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n})\in P</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="S2.p10.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=(\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}x_{i})/(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=(\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}x_{i})/(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that the point
<math id="S2.p10.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1}-s,x_{2}-s,\ldots,x_{n-1}-s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1}-s,x_{2}-s,\ldots,x_{n-1}-s)</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S2.p10.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, for any
<math id="S2.p10.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in\{1,2,\ldots,n-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in\{1,2,\ldots,n-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> the difference <math id="S2.p10.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i}-s)-(x_{j}-s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i}-s)-(x_{j}-s)</annotation></semantics></math> is the same
as <math id="S2.p10.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and every bound imposed on <math id="S2.p10.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p10.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
consequence of the linear inequalities defining <math id="S2.p10.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, we
have <math id="S2.p10.m61" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}(x_{i}-s)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}(x_{i}-s)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. (In particular, <math id="S2.p10.m62" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is not empty.)
Using any <math id="S2.p10.m63" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\operatorname{In}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>In</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\operatorname{In}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and any
<math id="S2.p10.m64" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\operatorname{Out}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Out</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\operatorname{Out}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, we may write</span></p>
