<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-R-M_{j,n}\leq x_{j}-s-M_{j,n}&lt;x_{n}-s&lt;x_{i}-s-m_{i,n}\leq R-s-m_{i,n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-R-M_{j,n}\leq x_{j}-s-M_{j,n}&lt;x_{n}-s&lt;x_{i}-s-m_{i,n}\leq R-s-m_{i,n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">As a consequence, <math id="S2.p10.m65" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n}-s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n}-s</annotation></semantics></math> is also bounded, there is an <math id="S2.p10.m66" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{*}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.p10.m67" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1}-s,x_{2}-s,\ldots,x_{n}-s)\in[-R^{*},R^{*}]^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1}-s,x_{2}-s,\ldots,x_{n}-s)\in[-R^{*},R^{*}]^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Observe finally that
<math id="S2.p10.m68" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S2.p10.m69" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)s+x_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)s+x_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S2.p10.m70" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n}-s=-ns" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n}-s=-ns</annotation></semantics></math>. Since
<math id="S2.p10.m71" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n}-s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n}-s</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded, so is <math id="S2.p10.m72" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=(x_{n}-s)/(-n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=(x_{n}-s)/(-n)</annotation></semantics></math> and the vector
<math id="S2.p10.m73" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, obtained by adding <math id="S2.p10.m74" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,s,\ldots,s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,s,\ldots,s)</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S2.p10.m75" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1}-s,x_{2}-s,\ldots,x_{n}-s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1}-s,x_{2}-s,\ldots,x_{n}-s)</annotation></semantics></math>, is also bounded.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A variant of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 2.10. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a> is stated in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Theorem
1.8]</cite>. As noted in Remark <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Remark 1.4. ‣ 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>,
the paper of Hopkins and Perkinson considers relative bounded regions
in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of bounded regions in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Their
result may be generalized to deformations of
graphical arrangements as follows: if we define the associated
weighted digraphs the same way as before, then relative bounded regions
correspond to digraphs whose strongly connected components are the same as the
weakly connected components. We leave the proof of this variant to the
reader.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The bounded regions of the Linial arrangement are in bijection with the
strongly connected semiacyclic tournaments.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.13</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Athanasiadis computed the number <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b({\mathcal{L}}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b({\mathcal{L}}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> of bounded regions
of the Linial arrangement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Theorem 4.2]</cite>
and raised the question whether there is a combinatorial interpretation
of the numbers <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b({\mathcal{L}}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b({\mathcal{L}}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> similar to the combinatorial
interpretation of the number of all regions given by Postnikov and
Stanley <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem12" title="Corollary 2.12. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12</span></a> provides a
new response to this question. Previous models were provided by
Tewari <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite> and by Flórez and Forge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, Theorem
2.2]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We may generalize our observations regarding the Linial arrangement
to an arbitrary deformed graphical arrangement <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.
Assume <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>).
Without loss of generality we may assume that for each ordered pair
<math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> the numbers <math id="S2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}^{(1)},a_{ij}^{(2)},\ldots,a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}^{(1)},a_{ij}^{(2)},\ldots,a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}</annotation></semantics></math> are listed in increasing order.
Each region of <math id="S2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighted digraphical polytope and
it is described by a system of inequalities (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) where each
<math id="S2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is either <math id="S2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> or some element of the set
<math id="S2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{ij}^{(1)},a_{ij}^{(2)},\ldots,a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{ij}^{(1)},a_{ij}^{(2)},\ldots,a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the following formula:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}=\begin{cases}a_{ij}^{(1)}&amp;\mbox{if $m_{ij}=-\infty$};\\
a_{ij}^{(k+1)}&amp;\mbox{if $m_{ij}=a_{ij}^{(k)}$ for some $k&lt;n_{ij}$};\\
\infty&amp;\mbox{if $m_{ij}=a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}$}.\\
\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mtext> for some </mtext><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}=\begin{cases}a_{ij}^{(1)}&amp;\mbox{if $m_{ij}=-\infty$};\\
a_{ij}^{(k+1)}&amp;\mbox{if $m_{ij}=a_{ij}^{(k)}$ for some $k&lt;n_{ij}$};\\
\infty&amp;\mbox{if $m_{ij}=a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}$}.\\
\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p11.2" class="ltx_p">Keeping in mind these possibilities, we define a <span id="S2.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">valid weighted
digraph</span> associated to a deformation of a graphical arrangement in
<math id="S2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> as
follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.14</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be a deformation of a graphical arrangement, given
by (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>). We call a weighted digraph with vertex set
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> valid if for each <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> exactly one of the following holds:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i,j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i,j}=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and there is no directed edge between </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i,j}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i,j}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, there is no directed edge </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and there
is exactly one directed edge </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> which has weight </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a_{ij}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a_{ij}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i,j}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i,j}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, there is a directed edge </span><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of weight
</span><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and there is exactly one directed edge </span><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
which has weight </span><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a_{ij}^{(k+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a_{ij}^{(k+1)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for some for some </span><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;n_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;n_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i,j}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i,j}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, there is exactly one directed edge </span><math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
which has weight </span><math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}^{(n_{i,j})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}^{(n_{i,j})}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and there is no directed edge
</span><math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Since the associated weighted digraphs contain at most one directed
edge <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> for any ordered pair <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>, we may uniquely
encode each such weighted digraph with a <span id="S2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weight function</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w:\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\times\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}\cup\{-\infty\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w:\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\times\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}\cup\{-\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p12.2" class="ltx_p">by setting <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> to be the weight of the directed edge <math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math>, if
<math id="S2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> is present in the weighted digraph and <math id="S2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
The <math id="S2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic condition may be then rephrased as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{1},i_{2})+w(i_{2},i_{3})+\cdots+w(i_{m-1},i_{m})+w(i_{m},i_{1})&lt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{1},i_{2})+w(i_{2},i_{3})+\cdots+w(i_{m-1},i_{m})+w(i_{m},i_{1})&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p12.3" class="ltx_p">must hold for any cyclic list <math id="S2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{1},i_{2},\ldots,i_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{1},i_{2},\ldots,i_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> of elements of
<math id="S2.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here we extend the rules of addition to <math id="S2.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}\cup\{-\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}\cup\{-\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Remark <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Remark 2.7. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>.
To simplify the notation in all subsequent proofs, we introduce the
shorthand notation</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{1},i_{2},\ldots,i_{m})=w(i_{1},i_{2})+w(i_{2},i_{3})+\cdots+w(i_{m-1},i_{m})+w(i_{m},i_{1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{1},i_{2},\ldots,i_{m})=w(i_{1},i_{2})+w(i_{2},i_{3})+\cdots+w(i_{m-1},i_{m})+w(i_{m},i_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p12.4" class="ltx_p">for the total weight of all directed edges along the closed walk
<math id="S2.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}\rightarrow i_{2}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{m}\rightarrow i_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}\rightarrow i_{2}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{m}\rightarrow i_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As a consequence of Theorems <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 2.5. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 2.10. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>
we have the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.15</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be a deformation of a graphical arrangement, given
by (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>). Then the regions of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> are in bijection
with the valid <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraphs on
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> in such a way that bounded regions correspond to
strongly connected valid <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraphs.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, each region created by the hyperplanes of <math id="S2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> may be
uniquely encoded by exactly one valid <math id="S2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraph of the
given form: for each <math id="S2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
<math id="S2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, the value of <math id="S2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to exactly one interval created by
the set <math id="S2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{ij}^{(1)},a_{ij}^{(2)},\ldots,a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{ij}^{(1)},a_{ij}^{(2)},\ldots,a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and
this interval is the same for all points of the same region. The
assignment of valid weighted digraphs to regions is thus injective. By
Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 2.5. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, exactly the <math id="S2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic valid weighted digraphs
encode sets of inequalities with a nonempty solution set, and by
Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 2.10. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a> exactly the strongly connected <math id="S2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic valid
weighted digraphs represent bounded regions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic property can be independently
verified within each strong component of the weighted digraph. Hence we
have the following structure theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.16</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume a deformation of a graphical arrangement given by (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>)
has the property that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{ij}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{ij}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then associated weighted digraph of any region may be
uniquely constructed as follows.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We fix an ordered set partition </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{1},N_{2},\ldots,N_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{1},N_{2},\ldots,N_{k})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of the set
</span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots n\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. The parts of the ordered set partition will be the
vertex sets of the strong components. For any </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> having the
property that the part containing </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> precedes the part containing </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
there is a of weight directed edge </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> but no directed edge
</span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. The label on </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> must be
</span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and it must be </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a_{ij}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a_{ij}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if </span><math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On each strong component </span><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> we independently select a
strongly connected valid </span><math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-acyclic weighted digraph.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">By our assumption each valid weighted digraph has at least one
directed edge between any two vertices, hence the strong components may
be linearly ordered in such a way that for any <math id="S2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in a smaller strong
component and for any <math id="S2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in a larger strong component there is a
directed edge <math id="S2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> but no directed edge <math id="S2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math>.
The weighting of these edges can only be as stated. As noted above,
the <math id="S2.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic property only needs to be verified on the strongly connected
components.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p16.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> Stanley considers a sequence
<math id="S2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=(\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=(\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math> of deformations
of the braid arrangement, such that each <math id="S2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperplane
arrangement in <math id="S2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and for each <math id="S2.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots\}</annotation></semantics></math>
he defines <math id="S2.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>S</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as the subcollection of hyperplanes
<math id="S2.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=c</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}\subseteq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}\subseteq S</annotation></semantics></math>. He
calls such a sequence <span id="S2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exponential</span> if the number
<math id="S2.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\mathcal{A}^{S}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>S</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\mathcal{A}^{S}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> of regions of <math id="S2.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>S</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on
<math id="S2.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=|S|</annotation></semantics></math> and it is the number <math id="S2.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\mathcal{A}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\mathcal{A}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> of regions of <math id="S2.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Introducing the exponential generating functions</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathcal{A}}(t)=\sum_{n\geq 0}r(\mathcal{A}_{n})\cdot\frac{t^{n}}{n!}\quad\mbox{and}\quad B_{\mathcal{A}}(t)=\sum_{n\geq 1}b(\mathcal{A}_{n})\cdot\frac{t^{n}}{n!}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathcal{A}}(t)=\sum_{n\geq 0}r(\mathcal{A}_{n})\cdot\frac{t^{n}}{n!}\quad\mbox{and}\quad B_{\mathcal{A}}(t)=\sum_{n\geq 1}b(\mathcal{A}_{n})\cdot\frac{t^{n}}{n!}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.2" class="ltx_p">for all, respectively the bounded regions, Stanley <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Theorem
1.2]</cite></p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\mathcal{A}}(t)=1-\frac{1}{R_{\mathcal{A}}(t)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\mathcal{A}}(t)=1-\frac{1}{R_{\mathcal{A}}(t)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.3" class="ltx_p">The outline of the proof cites Zaslavsky’s
formula (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>), Whitney’s formula <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> and the
exponential formula in enumerative combinatorics. Formula (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>)
may also be derived from Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem16" title="Theorem 2.16. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.16</span></a> as follows. Consider an
exponential sequence <math id="S2.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=(\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=(\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math>
of deformations of the braid arrangements. As a consequence of Zaslavsky’s
formula (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>), for each <math id="S2.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and for each <math id="S2.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> the number <math id="S2.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(\mathcal{A}^{S}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>S</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(\mathcal{A}^{S}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> of bounded regions of
<math id="S2.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>S</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> also depends only on
<math id="S2.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=|S|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=|S|</annotation></semantics></math> and it is the number <math id="S2.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(\mathcal{A}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(\mathcal{A}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> of bounded regions of
<math id="S2.p16.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. To construct an associated weighted digraph of a
region of <math id="S2.p16.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> we first fix an ordered set partition
<math id="S2.p16.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{1},N_{2},\ldots,N_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{1},N_{2},\ldots,N_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> of the set <math id="S2.p16.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. After fixing <math id="S2.p16.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
and the size <math id="S2.p16.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of each <math id="S2.p16.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of ways we can select an ordered
set partition is <math id="S2.p16.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{n_{1},n_{2},\ldots,n_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{n_{1},n_{2},\ldots,n_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the next step we
must select a strongly connected valid <math id="S2.p16.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraph on
each part of our set partition. There are <math id="S2.p16.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(\mathcal{A}_{n_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(\mathcal{A}_{n_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math> ways to
perform this step on the part <math id="S2.p16.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence we obtain</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\mathcal{A}_{n})=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}n_{1}+\cdots+n_{k}=n\\
n_{1},\ldots,n_{k}&gt;0\end{subarray}}\binom{n}{n_{1},n_{2},\ldots,n_{k}}\prod_{i=1}^{k}b(\mathcal{A}_{n_{i}})\quad\mbox{for all $n\geq 1$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\mathcal{A}_{n})=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}n_{1}+\cdots+n_{k}=n\\
n_{1},\ldots,n_{k}&gt;0\end{subarray}}\binom{n}{n_{1},n_{2},\ldots,n_{k}}\prod_{i=1}^{k}b(\mathcal{A}_{n_{i}})\quad\mbox{for all $n\geq 1$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.4" class="ltx_p">This formula implies <math id="S2.p16.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{A}(t)=\sum_{k\geq 0}B_{A}(t)^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{A}(t)=\sum_{k\geq 0}B_{A}(t)^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>The poset of gains and sparse deformations</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a deformation of a graphical arrangement given
by (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>) and one of its valid <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic associated
weighted digraphs. We may use the weights to define a partial order on
the vertex set <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a valid <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{D}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{D}j</annotation></semantics></math> if there is a directed path
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=i_{0}\rightarrow i_{1}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{k}=j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=i_{0}\rightarrow i_{1}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{k}=j</annotation></semantics></math> such that
the weight of each directed edge <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}\rightarrow i_{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}\rightarrow i_{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is
nonnegative. We call the set <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, ordered by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="&lt;_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> the
poset of gains induced by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The fact that <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{D}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{D}j</annotation></semantics></math> is a partial order is a direct consequence of
the <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic property stated in Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 2.5. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>. In terms
of Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Corollary 2.8. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>, <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{D}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{D}j</annotation></semantics></math> holds if we can find a directed
path from <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> such that
we do not lose money by walking from <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> using that path.
In general, the poset of gains carries less information than
recording the actual weights, but in some special cases all information
may be reconstructed from it. One example is the
Linial arrangement: its posets of gains are the <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sleek posets</span>,
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, Section
8.2]</cite>. Semiacyclic tournaments and sleek posets are
in bijection: for <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, the relation <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{D}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{D}j</annotation></semantics></math> holds exactly when the
<math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=-1</annotation></semantics></math> then
<math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> are incomparable. It has been shown in
of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, Theorem 8.6]</cite> that the length of a minimal
ascending cycle is at most <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, equivalently, sleek posets may be
characterized by a finite set of excluded subposets on at most <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>
elements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A similar approach may be taken to the <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semiorder arrangement</span>,
defined as the set of hyperplanes</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=-1,1\quad\mbox{for $1\leq i&lt;j\leq n$ in $V_{n-1}$.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mtext> in </mtext><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=-1,1\quad\mbox{for $1\leq i&lt;j\leq n$ in $V_{n-1}$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">For these, in any associated
valid weighted digraph, regardless of the order of the numbers <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, there is either a single directed edge of weight <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, or there are two edges of weight <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>, one in each
direction. The elements <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> are comparable in the poset
of gains exactly when there is a single directed
edge between them, pointing toward the larger
element. These are exactly the <span id="S3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semiorders</span> as defined
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, Section 7]</cite>. In analogy
to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, Theorem 8.6]</cite> one may directly show
the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In a valid weighted digraph associated to the semiorder arrangement, the
length of a shortest <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle is at most <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The equivalent statement for semiorders was first shown by Scott and
Suppes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. The Linial arrangement and the semiorder
arrangement are both examples of the following class of hyperplane arrangements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We call <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> a deformation of the braid arrangement, given
by (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>) sparse if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq n_{i,j}\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq n_{i,j}\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, and the signs of the numbers <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i,j}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i,j}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy
the following for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i,j}^{(1)}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i,j}^{(1)}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> holds, whenever </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i,j}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i,j}=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i,j}^{(1)}&lt;0&lt;a_{i,j}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i,j}^{(1)}&lt;0&lt;a_{i,j}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> holds, whenever
</span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i,j}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i,j}=2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We call <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> an interval order arrangement if
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i,j}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i,j}=2</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that interval order arrangements are precisely the hyperplane
arrangements associated to interval orders in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
Generalizing our observations made so far, we have the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a sparse deformation of the braid arrangement and any valid
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> associated to it. In the induced poset
of gains, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{D}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{D}j</annotation></semantics></math> holds exactly when there is a single directed
edge <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> of positive weight. For any pair <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}</annotation></semantics></math> of
incomparable vertices satisfying <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, the edge <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\rightarrow i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\rightarrow i</annotation></semantics></math> is
always present, and any edge between <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> has negative weight.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The straightforward verification is left to the reader. An interesting
property of sparse deformations of the braid arrangement is that a bounded
region corresponds to a poset of gains with a connected <span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">incomparability
graph</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The incomparability graph of a partially ordered set is the undirected
graph whose vertices are the elements of the poset, and the edges are
the incomparable pairs of elements.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be a valid <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraph associated to a sparse
deformation of the braid arrangement in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly
connected then the incomparability graph of the induced
poset of gains is connected. The converse is also true when
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i,j}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i,j}=2</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume first <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly connected. We show for any pair of vertices
<math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}</annotation></semantics></math> that there is a path between them in the incomparability graph
of <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="&lt;_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is nothing to prove if <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> are
incomparable. Without loss of generality we may assume that <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{D}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{D}j</annotation></semantics></math>
holds, by Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 3.4. ‣ 3. The poset of gains and sparse deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> this implies the presence of a
single directed edge <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly
connected, there is a path <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=i_{0}\rightarrow i_{1}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{k}=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=i_{0}\rightarrow i_{1}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{k}=i</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> in which each edge
<math id="S3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}\rightarrow i_{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}\rightarrow i_{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is either between a pair of incomparable
vertices, or corresponds to the relation <math id="S3.p7.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{s}&lt;_{D}i_{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}&lt;_{D}i_{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. It suffices
to show that in a shortest such path all edges correspond to
incomparable pairs. Assume, by contradiction, that <math id="S3.p7.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{s}&lt;_{D}i_{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}&lt;_{D}i_{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math> holds for some <math id="S3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}\rightarrow i_{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}\rightarrow i_{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider first the
case when this edge is succeeded by an edge
<math id="S3.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s+1}\rightarrow i_{s+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s+1}\rightarrow i_{s+2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The edge <math id="S3.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s+1}\rightarrow i_{s+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s+1}\rightarrow i_{s+2}</annotation></semantics></math> must
correspond to an incomparable pair of vertices, otherwise <math id="S3.p7.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{s}&lt;_{D}i_{s+1}&lt;_{D}i_{s+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}&lt;_{D}i_{s+1}&lt;_{D}i_{s+2}</annotation></semantics></math> holds, and the segment <math id="S3.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}\rightarrow i_{s+1}\rightarrow i_{s+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}\rightarrow i_{s+1}\rightarrow i_{s+2}</annotation></semantics></math> in
our path may be shortened to <math id="S3.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}\rightarrow i_{s+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}\rightarrow i_{s+2}</annotation></semantics></math>. A similar
shortening may also occur if the vertex <math id="S3.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s+2}</annotation></semantics></math> forms an incomparable
pair with <math id="S3.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, or if <math id="S3.p7.m25" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{s}&lt;_{D}i_{s+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}&lt;_{D}i_{s+2}</annotation></semantics></math> holds. Hence we must have
<math id="S3.p7.m26" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{s+2}&lt;_{D}i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s+2}&lt;_{D}i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m27" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{s+2}&lt;_{D}i_{s}&lt;_{D}i_{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s+2}&lt;_{D}i_{s}&lt;_{D}i_{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in contradiction
with the presence of the edge <math id="S3.p7.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s+1}\rightarrow i_{s+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s+1}\rightarrow i_{s+2}</annotation></semantics></math>. A similar
contradiction may be reached if <math id="S3.p7.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}\rightarrow i_{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}\rightarrow i_{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is preceded by
another edge. Hence we may have only one edge <math id="S3.p7.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\rightarrow i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\rightarrow i</annotation></semantics></math>
corresponding to <math id="S3.p7.m31" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j&lt;_{D}i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;_{D}i</annotation></semantics></math>, in contradiction with <math id="S3.p7.m32" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{D}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{D}j</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume now that <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i,j}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i,j}=2</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> and that the
incomparability graph of <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="&lt;_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. In this case the edges of
negative weight in <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained from the incomparability graph of
<math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="&lt;_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> by replacing each undirected edge of the incomparability graph by
a pair of opposing directed edges. Since the incomparability graph is
connected, there is a directed path using edges of negative weight only
from any vertex to any vertex in <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the Linial arrangement and the semiacyclic tournament <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>
containing a directed edge <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> for each
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a valid <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraph, it is in fact acyclic. The
induced poset of gains is an antichain, the
incomparability graph is the complete graph, it is connected. However,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is not strongly connected.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude this section with presenting a class of sparse deformations
of the braid arrangement for which the properties of the associated
valid <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraphs may be used to give an upper bound for the
number of regions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{a}=(a_{1},a_{2},\ldots,a_{n})\in{\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{a}=(a_{1},a_{2},\ldots,a_{n})\in{\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a
vector of nonnegative real numbers. We
define the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{a}</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized Linial arrangement as the set
of hyperplanes</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=a_{i}\quad\mbox{for $1\leq i&lt;j\leq n$ in $V_{n-1}$.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for </mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> in </mtext><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=a_{i}\quad\mbox{for $1\leq i&lt;j\leq n$ in $V_{n-1}$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Note that setting <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}=a_{2}=\cdots=a_{n}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}=a_{2}=\cdots=a_{n}=1</annotation></semantics></math> yields the
Linial arrangement. In any valid associated weighted digraph <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> there
is exactly one directed edge for each <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">either an edge <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math>, of weight <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to
<math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}&gt;a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}&gt;a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>: we call such an edge an <span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ascent</span>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">or an edge <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>: we call such an edge a <span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">descent</span>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.p10.2" class="ltx_p">As a consequence, the weighted digraph <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> may be uniquely reconstructed
from its underlying tournament. It depends on the values of the
parameters <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> which tournaments contain no <math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle, but
there is a bijection between the regions of an
<math id="S3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{a}</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized Linial arrangement and a set of tournaments
on <math id="S3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. That said, certain tournaments may be excluded
regardless of the choice of the
parameters. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, we call a cycle <span id="S3.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">alternating</span> if ascents and descents alternate in it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a valid <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraph associated to an
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{a}</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized Linial arrangement, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> contains no
alternating cycle.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given a directed cycle in a tournament on <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, let us call a
vertex <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> a </span><span id="S3.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">peak</span><span id="S3.p11.1.3" class="ltx_text"> if an descent follows a ascent at <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> along the
cycle, and let us call <math id="S3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> a </span><span id="S3.p11.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">valley</span><span id="S3.p11.1.5" class="ltx_text"> if ascent follows a descent at
<math id="S3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Peaks and valleys alternate along an alternating cycle, hence we
may compute the total weight of its edges by counting the contribution
of each edge at the valley incident to it. The incoming edge at a valley
<math id="S3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> has weight <math id="S3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the outgoing edge has weight <math id="S3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the
total weight of all edges is zero, regardless of the values of the
parameters <math id="S3.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. An alternating cycle is <math id="S3.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">It has been shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, Theorem 4.4]</cite> that the number of
alternation acyclic tournaments on the set <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the median
Genocchi number <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The number of regions in any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{a}</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized Linial
arrangement in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is less than or equal to the median
Genocchi number <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It has been shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> that there is a bijection
between alternation acyclic tournaments and the regions of the homogenized Linial arrangement whose hyperplanes are defined by
equations of the form <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>s are also
coordinate functions. Hence we may think of an
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{a}</annotation></semantics></math>-generalized Linial arrangement as the intersection of
the homogenized Linial arrangement with the hyperplanes <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math>. Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem10" title="Corollary 3.10. ‣ 3. The poset of gains and sparse deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a> may also be shown using
these observations.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The bigraphical arrangements introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> are
related to the sparse deformations discussed in this section.
Given a simple graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="G=(\{1,2\ldots,n\},E\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(\{1,2\ldots,n\},E\})</annotation></semantics></math>, for each edge <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}</annotation></semantics></math> we
choose real parameters <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j,i}</annotation></semantics></math>, such
that there is an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying all inequalities of the form
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;a_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;a_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}-x_{i}&lt;a_{j,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}-x_{i}&lt;a_{j,i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The bigraphical arrangement is the set of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2|E|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2|E|</annotation></semantics></math>
hyperplanes <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}-x_{j}=a_{i,j}\&gt;:\&gt;\{i,j\}\in E\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}-x_{j}=a_{i,j}\&gt;:\&gt;\{i,j\}\in E\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a_{j,i}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;a_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a_{j,i}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;a_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> must have a solution,
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i,j}+a_{j,i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i,j}+a_{j,i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> must hold for each <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}\in E</annotation></semantics></math>.
The valid associated weighted digraphs may be described as follows. For
each edge <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}\in E</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, exactly one of the following
possibilities hold:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There a directed edge </span><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of weight
</span><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j,i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (representing </span><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;-a_{j,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;-a_{j,i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a directed edge </span><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of weight </span><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a_{j,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a_{j,i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
and a directed edge </span><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of weight </span><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
(representing </span><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a_{j,i}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;a_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a_{j,i}&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;a_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There a directed edge </span><math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of weight
</span><math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i,}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (representing </span><math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}&gt;a_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}&gt;a_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is part of the definition of a bigraphical arrangement that selecting
option (2) on each <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}\in E</annotation></semantics></math> should yield an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic
digraph (labeling the central region).
The definition of a partial orientation
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> is equivalent to the above weighted
digraph labeling. The oriented edges in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> are
directed in the opposite way, the unoriented edges correspond to our
pairs of opposing directed edges. The definition of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-admissibility is
equivalent to our <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic condition (the score used
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> is the negative of our weight) and
potential cycles are directed cycles using some directed edges
whose opposite is also present. The same observations also apply to the
mixed graphs appearing in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Separated deformations and the weak triangle inequality</h2>

<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be a deformation of the braid arrangement, given
by (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>). We call the arrangement <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>
separated if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the set <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{ij}^{(1)},a_{ij}^{(2)},\ldots,a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{ij}^{(1)},a_{ij}^{(2)},\ldots,a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In any valid associated weighted digraph of a separated
deformation <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of the braid arrangement given
by (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>) either <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,i)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,i)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> holds
for each <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, by our assumptions <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i,j}^{(1)}\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i,j}^{(1)}\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math> (and hence <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-a_{i,j}^{(1)}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-a_{i,j}^{(1)}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}^{(n_{ij})}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> hold, furthermore, for each
<math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;n_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;n_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, the numbers <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}^{(k+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}^{(k+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> can not be
both nonzero real numbers, having opposite signs. The statement is a
direct consequence of Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem14" title="Definition 2.14. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a separated deformation of the braid arrangement, the induced poset of gains
associated to any valid <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraph is a totally ordered set.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Hence there is a unique permutation <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma=\sigma(1)\sigma(2)\cdots\sigma(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=\sigma(1)\sigma(2)\cdots\sigma(n)</annotation></semantics></math> of the coordinates such that <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\sigma(i),\sigma(j))\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\sigma(i),\sigma(j))\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>
holds for all <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We call the permutation <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> the order of gains associated to
the valid <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraph of a separated deformation of the
braid arrangement. We will also use the notation <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j</annotation></semantics></math> to
indicate that the label <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> precedes the label <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">So far we only rephrased the observation that for a separated
deformation of the braid arrangement, each region is included in a
region <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma(1)}&gt;x_{\sigma(2)}&gt;\cdots&gt;x_{\sigma(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma(1)}&gt;x_{\sigma(2)}&gt;\cdots&gt;x_{\sigma(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>
of the braid arrangement. Using Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 2.10. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a> we may refine this
observation as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a region of a separated deformation of the braid
arrangement and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma(1)}&gt;x_{\sigma(2)}&gt;\cdots&gt;x_{\sigma(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma(1)}&gt;x_{\sigma(2)}&gt;\cdots&gt;x_{\sigma(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>
be the unique region of the braid arrangement containing it. Then there
is a unique sequence <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=i_{0}\leq i_{1}&lt;i_{2}&lt;\cdots&lt;i_{k}=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=i_{0}\leq i_{1}&lt;i_{2}&lt;\cdots&lt;i_{k}=n</annotation></semantics></math> such that
writing <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> as the concatenation</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma=(\sigma(i_{0})\cdots\sigma(i_{1}))\cdot(\sigma(i_{1}+1)\cdots\sigma(i_{2}))\cdots(\sigma(i_{k-1}+1)\cdots\sigma(i_{k}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=(\sigma(i_{0})\cdots\sigma(i_{1}))\cdot(\sigma(i_{1}+1)\cdots\sigma(i_{2}))\cdots(\sigma(i_{k-1}+1)\cdots\sigma(i_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">of contiguous subwords has the following properties:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=-1,0,\ldots,k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=-1,0,\ldots,k-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> the intersection of </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
with the linear span of </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{\sigma(i_{j}+1)},e_{\sigma(i_{j}+2)},\ldots,e_{\sigma(i_{j+1})}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{\sigma(i_{j}+1)},e_{\sigma(i_{j}+2)},\ldots,e_{\sigma(i_{j+1})}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a bounded region.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If a subset </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contains indices </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and
</span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(j_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(j_{1})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(j_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(j_{2})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> belong to different
subwords in the above decomposition then the intersection of </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
with the linear span of </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{\sigma(j)}\&gt;:\&gt;j\in S\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{\sigma(j)}\&gt;:\&gt;j\in S\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is unbounded.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 2.10. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>, for any nonempty subset <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> the intersection of <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the linear
span of <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{\sigma(j)}\&gt;:\&gt;j\in S\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{\sigma(j)}\&gt;:\&gt;j\in S\}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded if the
restriction of the weighted digraph <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to
<math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the set <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\sigma(j)\&gt;:\&gt;j\in S\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.498em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\sigma(j)\&gt;:\&gt;j\in S\}</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly
connected. Note that the converse is not necessarily true: inequalities implied
by edges not belonging to the restriction may force the intersection to
be bounded even if the restriction is not strongly connected.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">First we show the existence of such a decomposition.
Let <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the largest index such that there is a directed
path from <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular we set <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)1</annotation></semantics></math>
has indegree zero in <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Keep in mind that the
definition of <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> includes the equality <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}+\cdots+x_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}+\cdots+x_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
together with <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma(2)}=\cdots=x_{\sigma(n)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma(2)}=\cdots=x_{\sigma(n)}=0</annotation></semantics></math> this forces
<math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma(1)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma(1)}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Since there is a
directed path <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)\rightarrow\sigma(2)\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow\sigma(i_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)\rightarrow\sigma(2)\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow\sigma(i_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction of
<math id="S4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the set
<math id="S4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]=\{\sigma(1),\sigma(2),\ldots,\sigma(i_{1})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]=\{\sigma(1),\sigma(2),\ldots,\sigma(i_{1})\}</annotation></semantics></math> is
strongly connected. Furthermore, given any <math id="S4.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1}\in[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1}\in[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]</annotation></semantics></math> and
any <math id="S4.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{2}\not\in[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{2}\not\in[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]</annotation></semantics></math>, no directed edge
<math id="S4.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{2}\rightarrow j_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{2}\rightarrow j_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can exist as <math id="S4.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is not reachable from
<math id="S4.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence any subset of <math id="S4.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]</annotation></semantics></math> and any subset of
its complement must belong to different strong components: once we leave
<math id="S4.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]</annotation></semantics></math>, there is no arrow we could use to return to
it. As seen in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 2.10. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>, this implies that all
(implied) differences of the form <math id="S4.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma(j_{1})}-x_{\sigma(j_{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma(j_{1})}-x_{\sigma(j_{2})}</annotation></semantics></math> are only
bounded from below in the definition of <math id="S4.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> which remains
unbounded even after intersecting it with the linear span of
<math id="S4.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{\sigma(j)}\&gt;:\&gt;j\in S\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{\sigma(j)}\&gt;:\&gt;j\in S\}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> containing both <math id="S4.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We may continue finding a suitable <math id="S4.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, …<math id="S4.p5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in
a recursive fashion: for <math id="S4.p5.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,2,\ldots,k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,2,\ldots,k-1</annotation></semantics></math>, we may define <math id="S4.p5.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math> as
the largest index such that <math id="S4.p5.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> may be reached from
<math id="S4.p5.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i_{j+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i_{j+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">To show the uniqueness of our decomposition, observe first that replacing <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
with any larger <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> results in a subset
<math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1}^{\prime})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1}^{\prime})]</annotation></semantics></math> such that the restriction of
<math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1}^{\prime})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1}^{\prime})]</annotation></semantics></math> is not strongly
connected: for any <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1}\leq i_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1}\leq i_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{2}&gt;i_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{2}&gt;i_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> there is only a directed edge
from <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(j_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(j_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(j_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(j_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. As above, using the proof of
Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 2.10. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>, we may conclude that the
differences of the form <math id="S4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma(j_{1})}-x_{\sigma(j_{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma(j_{1})}-x_{\sigma(j_{2})}</annotation></semantics></math> are only
bounded from below in the definition of <math id="S4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the
intersection of <math id="S4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with the linear span of
<math id="S4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{\sigma(j)}\&gt;:\&gt;1\leq j\leq i_{1}^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{\sigma(j)}\&gt;:\&gt;1\leq j\leq i_{1}^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is still unbounded. On the
other hand, for any <math id="S4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}^{\prime}&lt;i_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}^{\prime}&lt;i_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the set <math id="S4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]</annotation></semantics></math> properly
contains <math id="S4.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1}^{\prime})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1}^{\prime})]</annotation></semantics></math> and the restriction of
<math id="S4.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma(1),\sigma(i_{1})]</annotation></semantics></math> is strongly
connected.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Next we define the <span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gain
function <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> associated to a
valid <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraph as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> we define the gain function
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\sigma(i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\sigma(i))</annotation></semantics></math> as the maximum weight of a directed path beginning at
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and ending at <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we set
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\sigma(1))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\sigma(1))=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the total order of gains.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The word “gain” here is used as the opposite of “cost” in a weighted
directed graph setting and it is not related to the notion of a gain
graph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every gain function has the weakly increasing property</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\sigma(1))\leq g(\sigma(2))\leq\cdots\leq g(\sigma(n))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\sigma(1))\leq g(\sigma(2))\leq\cdots\leq g(\sigma(n)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, for each <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, every maximum weight directed path from
<math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i)</annotation></semantics></math> may be extended to a directed walk to
<math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(j)</annotation></semantics></math> by adding the directed edge <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i)\rightarrow\sigma(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i)\rightarrow\sigma(j)</annotation></semantics></math>
of nonnegative weight. Eliminating cycles from the walk can only
increase the total weight, hence we have</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\sigma(j))\geq g(\sigma(i))+w(\sigma(i),\sigma(j))\geq g(\sigma(i))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\sigma(j))\geq g(\sigma(i))+w(\sigma(i),\sigma(j))\geq g(\sigma(i)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.2" class="ltx_p">The question naturally arises: could the maximum gain be achieved by
using directed edges of nonnegative weight only? In the rest of this
section we show that the answer is yes at least in an important special
case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We call a deformation <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of the braid arrangement integral if all the numbers <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i,j}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i,j}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in
(<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>) are integers. We say that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the
weak triangle inequality if for all triplets <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j,k)</annotation></semantics></math>, the
inequalities <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,k)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,k)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> imply</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)\leq w(i,j)+w(j,k)+1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)\leq w(i,j)+w(j,k)+1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in any valid <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic associated weighted digraph.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.10</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be a separated integral deformation of the braid
arrangement satisfying the weak triangle inequality, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be an
associated <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraph. Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be the total order
of gains associated to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> be the gain function. Then, for
each <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> there is a directed path from <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i)</annotation></semantics></math> such
that all weights in the path are nonnegative and the total weight of the
edges in the path is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\sigma(i))-g(\sigma(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\sigma(i))-g(\sigma(1))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider a weighted path <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)=i_{0}\rightarrow i_{1}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{m}=\sigma(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)=i_{0}\rightarrow i_{1}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{m}=\sigma(i)</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i)</annotation></semantics></math> such
that the weight of the path is <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\sigma(i))-g(\sigma(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\sigma(i))-g(\sigma(1))</annotation></semantics></math> and it has the least possible
number of negative weighted edges. We are done if there is no edge of
negative weight, otherwise assume that <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{k}\rightarrow i_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{k}\rightarrow i_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the
first edge satisfying <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{k},i_{k+1})&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{k},i_{k+1})&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. All preceding steps being of
nonnegative weight, we must have <math id="S4.p9.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{0}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}\cdots&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}\cdots&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The inequality <math id="S4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{k},i_{k+1})&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{k},i_{k+1})&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> implies
<math id="S4.p9.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{k+1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{k+1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence there is a unique <math id="S4.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq k-1</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="S4.p9.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{j}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{k+1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{j+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{k+1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math>
holds. (Recall that, the <math id="S4.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic property implies that walks
revisiting a vertex can only have lower weight than the path obtained by
eliminating the closed subwalks, hence the inequalities must be strict.)
Applying the weak triangle inequality to he triple <math id="S4.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{j},i_{k+1},i_{j+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{j},i_{k+1},i_{j+1})</annotation></semantics></math> we
obtain</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{j},i_{j+1})\leq w(i_{j},i_{k+1})+w(i_{k+1},i_{j+1})+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{j},i_{j+1})\leq w(i_{j},i_{k+1})+w(i_{k+1},i_{j+1})+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">Let us add the weight <math id="S4.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{w}(i_{j+1},i_{j+2},\ldots,i_{k},i_{k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{w}(i_{j+1},i_{j+2},\ldots,i_{k},i_{k+1})</annotation></semantics></math> of the walk
<math id="S4.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j+1}\rightarrow i_{j+2}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{k}\rightarrow i_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j+1}\rightarrow i_{j+2}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{k}\rightarrow i_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> to both sides. On the left hand side we obtain the weight of the walk
<math id="S4.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}\rightarrow i_{j+1}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{k}\rightarrow i_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}\rightarrow i_{j+1}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{k}\rightarrow i_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{w}(i_{j},i_{j+1},\ldots i_{k},i_{k+1})\leq w(i_{j},i_{k+1})+\vec{w}(i_{j+1},i_{j+2},\ldots,i_{k},i_{k+1})+w(i_{k+1},i_{j+1})+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{w}(i_{j},i_{j+1},\ldots i_{k},i_{k+1})\leq w(i_{j},i_{k+1})+\vec{w}(i_{j+1},i_{j+2},\ldots,i_{k},i_{k+1})+w(i_{k+1},i_{j+1})+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">The sum <math id="S4.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{w}(i_{j+1},i_{j+2},\ldots,i_{k},i_{k+1})+w(i_{k+1},i_{j+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{w}(i_{j+1},i_{j+2},\ldots,i_{k},i_{k+1})+w(i_{k+1},i_{j+1})</annotation></semantics></math> on
the right hand side is the weight of the closed walk
<math id="S4.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j+1}\rightarrow i_{j+1}\rightarrow i_{j+1}\rightarrow i_{j+2}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{k}\rightarrow i_{k+1}\rightarrow i_{j+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j+1}\rightarrow i_{j+1}\rightarrow i_{j+1}\rightarrow i_{j+2}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{k}\rightarrow i_{k+1}\rightarrow i_{j+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is negative by the <math id="S4.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic property, and it is at
most <math id="S4.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> by the integrality of <math id="S4.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vec{w}(i_{j},i_{j+1},\ldots i_{k},i_{k+1})\leq w(i_{j},i_{k+1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vec{w}(i_{j},i_{j+1},\ldots i_{k},i_{k+1})\leq w(i_{j},i_{k+1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p9.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.4.1" class="ltx_text">which means that we may replace the subpath <math id="S4.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}\rightarrow i_{j+1}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{k}\rightarrow i_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}\rightarrow i_{j+1}\rightarrow\cdots\rightarrow i_{k}\rightarrow i_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> with the
nonnegative edge <math id="S4.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}\rightarrow i_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}\rightarrow i_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus decreasing the number of
negative edges without decreasing the total weight, in contradiction
with our assumptions.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">An example of a saturated integral arrangement in which
<math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\sigma(i))-g(\sigma(1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\sigma(i))-g(\sigma(1))</annotation></semantics></math> is attained only by a path containing a
negative weighted edge is given in Example <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem12" title="Example 5.12. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a>.
<span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">From now on until the rest of the section we only consider separated
integral deformations of the braid arrangement satisfying the weak
triangle inequality.</span>
If we know the total order of gains, Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 4.10. ‣ 4. Separated deformations and the weak triangle inequality ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a> allows
us to compute the gain function in a greedy fashion:</p>
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We set <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\sigma(1))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\sigma(1))=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once we computed <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\sigma(1))),g(\sigma(2)),\ldots,g(\sigma(i-1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\sigma(1))),g(\sigma(2)),\ldots,g(\sigma(i-1))</annotation></semantics></math>, the value of <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\sigma(i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\sigma(i))</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\sigma(i))=\max_{1\leq j\leq i-1}(g(\sigma(j)+w(\sigma(j),\sigma(i))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\sigma(i))=\max_{1\leq j\leq i-1}(g(\sigma(j)+w(\sigma(j),\sigma(i)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.p10.2" class="ltx_p">The choice of <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in equation (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In item 2 ‣ 4. Separated deformations and the weak triangle inequality ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) may not be unique. We
eliminate the ambiguity by always selecting the rightmost possible label
<math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(j)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.11</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We select the largest <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In item 2 ‣ 4. Separated deformations and the weak triangle inequality ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) and call the
resulting <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(j)</annotation></semantics></math> the parent of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i)</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\sigma(i))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\sigma(i))</annotation></semantics></math>. We extend the definition to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)</annotation></semantics></math> by setting
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\sigma(1))=\sigma(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\sigma(1))=\sigma(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the pairs <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma(i),p(\sigma(i)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma(i),p(\sigma(i)))</annotation></semantics></math> form the edges of a tree
rooted at <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. We call this rooted tree the <span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tree of the
gain function</span>. In the study of the properties of this tree the
following lemma plays a key role.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.12</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(j)&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(j)&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i</annotation></semantics></math> hold then we have
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=g(j)-g(i)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=g(j)-g(i)-1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(p(j),i)=g(i)-g(p(j))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(p(j),i)=g(i)-g(p(j))</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(j)\leq_{\sigma^{-1}}p(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(j)\leq_{\sigma^{-1}}p(i)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">By our assumption <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is to the right of <math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(j)</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)\leq g(j)-g(i)-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)\leq g(j)-g(i)-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">By (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4. Separated deformations and the weak triangle inequality ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) we also have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(p(j),i)\leq g(i)-g(p(j))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(p(j),i)\leq g(i)-g(p(j)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p12.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.3.1" class="ltx_text">The sum of the two inequalities, combined with the weak triangle
inequality yields</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(j)-g(p(j))=w(p(j),j)\leq w(p(j),i)+w(i,j)+1\leq g(j)-g(p(j))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(j)-g(p(j))=w(p(j),j)\leq w(p(j),i)+w(i,j)+1\leq g(j)-g(p(j)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p12.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.4.1" class="ltx_text">The left and the right end being equal all inequalities above must be
equalities. Hence we have <math id="S4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=g(j)-g(i)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=g(j)-g(i)-1</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(p(j),i)=g(i)-g(p(j))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(p(j),i)=g(i)-g(p(j))</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally <math id="S4.p12.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(j)\leq_{\sigma^{-1}}p(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(j)\leq_{\sigma^{-1}}p(i)</annotation></semantics></math> is a
direct consequence of <math id="S4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(p(j),i)=g(i)-g(p(j))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(p(j),i)=g(i)-g(p(j))</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.13</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The edges of the tree of the gain function are noncrossing: there is no
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(i_{1})&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}p(i_{2})&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(i_{1})&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}p(i_{2})&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> would hold.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume the contrary. By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem12" title="Lemma 4.12. ‣ 4. Separated deformations and the weak triangle inequality ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.12</span></a>, <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(i_{2})&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(i_{2})&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> imply <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(i_{2})\leq_{\sigma^{-1}}p(i_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(i_{2})\leq_{\sigma^{-1}}p(i_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>,
in contradiction with <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(i_{1})&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}p(i_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(i_{1})&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}p(i_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.14</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The number of possible types of trees of the gain function is a Catalan
number.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Contiguous integral deformations</h2>

<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We call an integral deformation of the braid arrangement in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> contiguous if, for every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, the set <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i,j}^{(1)},a_{i,j}^{(2)},\ldots,a_{i,j}^{(n_{i,j})}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i,j}^{(1)},a_{i,j}^{(2)},\ldots,a_{i,j}^{(n_{i,j})}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a contiguous set
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\alpha(i,j),\beta(i,j)]=\{\alpha(i,j),\alpha(i,j)+1,\ldots,\beta(i,j)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha(i,j),\beta(i,j)]=\{\alpha(i,j),\alpha(i,j)+1,\ldots,\beta(i,j)\}</annotation></semantics></math>
of integers.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the equation <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=c</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the equation
<math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}-x_{i}=-c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}-x_{i}=-c</annotation></semantics></math>, we may consistently extend our notation by setting</p>
<table id="S5.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(j,i)=-\beta(i,j)\quad\mbox{and}\quad\beta(j,i)=-\alpha(i,j)\quad\mbox{for $1\leq i&lt;j\leq n$.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(j,i)=-\beta(i,j)\quad\mbox{and}\quad\beta(j,i)=-\alpha(i,j)\quad\mbox{for $1\leq i&lt;j\leq n$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The truncated affine arrangements <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,b}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,b}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
are contiguous deformations of the braid
arrangement: we have <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(i,j)=1-a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(i,j)=1-a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)=b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)=b-1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We may specialize Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem14" title="Definition 2.14. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a> to such arrangements as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be a contiguous integral deformation of the braid
arrangement in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by the intervals
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\alpha(i,j),\beta(i,j)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha(i,j),\beta(i,j)]</annotation></semantics></math>. A valid associated digraph on the vertex set
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies for each <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> exactly one of the following:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is no directed edge </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and there
is a directed edge </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of weight </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\alpha(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\alpha(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a directed edge </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of weight
</span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and there is a directed edge </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of
weight </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-w-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-w-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for some for some integer </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in[\alpha(i,j),\beta(i,j)-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in[\alpha(i,j),\beta(i,j)-1]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a directed edge </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of weight
</span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and there is no directed edge </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As we did right after Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem14" title="Definition 2.14. ‣ 2. Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a>, we extend the weight
function to a function
<math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w:\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\times\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}\cup\{-\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w:\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\times\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\rightarrow{\mathbb{R}}\cup\{-\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> by setting the following for all <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ol id="S5.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The weight <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the set
<math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\alpha(i,j),\beta(i,j)]\cup\{-\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha(i,j),\beta(i,j)]\cup\{-\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the weight <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,i)</annotation></semantics></math> belongs
to the set
<math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-\beta(i,j),-\alpha(i,j)]\cup\{-\infty\}=[\alpha(j,i),\beta(j,i)]\cup\{-\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-\beta(i,j),-\alpha(i,j)]\cup\{-\infty\}=[\alpha(j,i),\beta(j,i)]\cup\{-\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,i)=-\alpha(i,j)=\beta(j,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,i)=-\alpha(i,j)=\beta(j,i)</annotation></semantics></math> and if
<math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=\beta(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=\beta(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,i)=-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,i)=-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all other values of <math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S5.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,i)=-1-w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,i)=-1-w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In other words, regardless of the order of <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the set
<math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\alpha(i,j),\beta(i,j)]\cup\{-\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha(i,j),\beta(i,j)]\cup\{-\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> holds
if and only if <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,i)=\beta(j,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,i)=\beta(j,i)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As we will see in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 5.13. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a> below, the <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic
property is especially easy to check for truncated affine arrangements
satisfying <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|a-b|\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|a-b|\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The following lemma still holds for all
contiguous integral deformations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=(i_{0},i_{1},\ldots,i_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=(i_{0},i_{1},\ldots,i_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle of minimum length
in a valid weighted digraph associated to a contiguous integral deformations of
the braid arrangement. Then for any diagonal pair of
vertices of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, for any <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{r},i_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{r},i_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> are
not cyclically consecutive, the associated weight function satisfies
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty\in\{w(i_{r},i_{s}),w(i_{s},i_{r})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty\in\{w(i_{r},i_{s}),w(i_{s},i_{r})\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume by contradiction that there is a pair <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{r},i_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{r},i_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of
cyclically not consecutive vertices such that
<math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty\not\in\{w(i_{r},i_{s}),w(i_{s},i_{r})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty\not\in\{w(i_{r},i_{s}),w(i_{s},i_{r})\}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case the real numbers
<math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{r},i_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{r},i_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{s},i_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{s},i_{r})</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{r},i_{s})+w(i_{s},i_{r})=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{r},i_{s})+w(i_{s},i_{r})=-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The cyclic lists <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{r},i_{r+1},\ldots,i_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{r},i_{r+1},\ldots,i_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{s},i_{s+1},\ldots,i_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{s},i_{s+1},\ldots,i_{r})</annotation></semantics></math> represent shorter closed
walks in the associated weighted digraph, which can not be
<math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending. Since all weights are integers we must have</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{r},i_{r+1},\ldots,i_{s})\leq-1\quad\mbox{and}\quad w(i_{s},i_{s+1},\ldots,i_{r})\leq-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{r},i_{r+1},\ldots,i_{s})\leq-1\quad\mbox{and}\quad w(i_{s},i_{s+1},\ldots,i_{r})\leq-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Taking the sum of these inequalities, after subtracting
<math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{r},i_{s})+w(i_{s},i_{r})=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{r},i_{s})+w(i_{s},i_{r})=-1</annotation></semantics></math> on both sides yields</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(C)=w(i_{r},i_{r+1},\ldots,i_{s})+w(i_{s},i_{s+1},\ldots,i_{r})-(w(i_{r},i_{s})+w(i_{s},i_{r}))\leq-1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(C)=w(i_{r},i_{r+1},\ldots,i_{s})+w(i_{s},i_{s+1},\ldots,i_{r})-(w(i_{r},i_{s})+w(i_{s},i_{r}))\leq-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">in contradiction with the closed walk represented by <math id="S5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> being
<math id="S5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The next theorem is a generalization of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, Theorem
8.6]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a contiguous integral deformation of the braid arrangement in
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S5.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,k)\leq\beta(i,j)+\beta(j,k)+1\quad\mbox{for all triplets $\{i,j,k\}$.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all triplets </mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,k)\leq\beta(i,j)+\beta(j,k)+1\quad\mbox{for all triplets $\{i,j,k\}$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then any valid associated weighted digraph is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic if and only if
it contains no <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle of length at most four.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">It suffices to show that
there is no valid associated weighted digraph in which a shortest <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending
cycle of length <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math> would exist. Assume by contradiction that
<math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=(i_{0},i_{1},\ldots,i_{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=(i_{0},i_{1},\ldots,i_{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math> is such an is an <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle.
Recall that, by Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, only one of the directed edges
<math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}\rightarrow i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}\rightarrow i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}\leftarrow i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}\leftarrow i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is present for each
diagonal pair <math id="S5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{r},i_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{r},i_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us call <math id="S5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}\in\{i_{0},i_{1},\ldots,i_{k-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}\in\{i_{0},i_{1},\ldots,i_{k-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> a </span><span id="S5.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">diagonal tail (diagonal head) vertex</span><span id="S5.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text"> if
<math id="S5.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}\rightarrow i_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}\rightarrow i_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S5.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}\leftarrow i_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}\leftarrow i_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>) holds for some diagonal pair
<math id="S5.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{r},i_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{r},i_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly, for <math id="S5.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, each vertex <math id="S5.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is either
a diagonal tail or a diagonal head vertex, some of them may be both. If
each <math id="S5.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}\in\{i_{0},i_{1},\ldots,i_{k-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}\in\{i_{0},i_{1},\ldots,i_{k-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is either only a diagonal
tail or only a diagonal, then we may reach a contradiction as follows.
At least one <math id="S5.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> must be a diagonal head, without loss of generality
we may assume <math id="S5.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we must have <math id="S5.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{2}\rightarrow i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{2}\rightarrow i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{3}\rightarrow i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{3}\rightarrow i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, …, <math id="S5.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{k-2}\rightarrow i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{k-2}\rightarrow i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, as a consequence
<math id="S5.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{k-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{k-2}</annotation></semantics></math> are diagonal tails and by <math id="S5.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math> the pair
<math id="S5.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{2},i_{k-2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{2},i_{k-2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a diagonal pair for which neither <math id="S5.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{2}\rightarrow i_{k-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{2}\rightarrow i_{k-2}</annotation></semantics></math> nor <math id="S5.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{2}\leftarrow i_{k-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{2}\leftarrow i_{k-2}</annotation></semantics></math> can hold, in contradiction with
Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>. Hence there is at least one vertex
<math id="S5.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}\in\{i_{0},i_{1},\ldots,i_{k-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}\in\{i_{0},i_{1},\ldots,i_{k-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> which is simultaneously a diagonal head
and a diagonal tail: <math id="S5.p7.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}\leftarrow i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}\leftarrow i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p7.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}\rightarrow i_{s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}\rightarrow i_{s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>
hold for some <math id="S5.p7.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,s^{\prime}\in\{0,1,\ldots,k-1\}-\{r-1,r,r+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,s^{\prime}\in\{0,1,\ldots,k-1\}-\{r-1,r,r+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Additions
and subtractions in the subscripts are performed modulo <math id="S5.p7.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.)
Without loss of generality we may assume that <math id="S5.p7.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p7.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are
cyclically consecutive.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1:</span><span id="S5.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text"> <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}=s+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}=s+1</annotation></semantics></math> holds. The cycles
<math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{r},i_{r+1},\ldots,i_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{r},i_{r+1},\ldots,i_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{s^{\prime}},i_{s^{\prime}+1},\ldots,\ldots i_{r-1},i_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{s^{\prime}},i_{s^{\prime}+1},\ldots,\ldots i_{r-1},i_{r})</annotation></semantics></math>
are shorter than <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, hence they can not be <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{r},i_{r+1},\ldots,i_{s})\leq-1\quad\mbox{and}\quad w(i_{s^{\prime}},i_{s^{\prime}+1},\ldots,i_{r})\leq-1\quad\mbox{must hold}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>must hold</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{r},i_{r+1},\ldots,i_{s})\leq-1\quad\mbox{and}\quad w(i_{s^{\prime}},i_{s^{\prime}+1},\ldots,i_{r})\leq-1\quad\mbox{must hold}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">Adding <math id="S5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{s},i_{s^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{s},i_{s^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> on both sides to the sum of the above
inequalities we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{s},i_{r})+w(i_{r},i_{s}^{\prime})+w(C)\leq-2+w(i_{s},i_{s^{\prime}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{s},i_{r})+w(i_{r},i_{s}^{\prime})+w(C)\leq-2+w(i_{s},i_{s^{\prime}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(C)</annotation></semantics></math> is nonnegative, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S5.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{s},i_{r})+w(i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}})\leq-2+w(i_{s},i_{s^{\prime}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{s},i_{r})+w(i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}})\leq-2+w(i_{s},i_{s^{\prime}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{s},i_{r}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{s},i_{r}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are diagonals, we have
<math id="S5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{s},i_{r})=\beta(i_{s},i_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{s},i_{r})=\beta(i_{s},i_{r})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}})=\beta(i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}})=\beta(i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore <math id="S5.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{s},i_{s^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{s},i_{s^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> is at most
<math id="S5.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{s},i_{s^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{s},i_{s^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Equation (<a href="#S5.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>) implies</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{s},i_{r})+\beta(i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}})\leq-2+\beta(i_{s},i_{s^{\prime}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{s},i_{r})+\beta(i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}})\leq-2+\beta(i_{s},i_{s^{\prime}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.5.1" class="ltx_text">in contradiction with (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2:</span><span id="S5.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text"> <math id="S5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\prime}=s-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\prime}=s-1</annotation></semantics></math> holds. This case is analogous
to the previous one. Now the cycles <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{r},i_{r+1},\ldots,i_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{r},i_{r+1},\ldots,i_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{s^{\prime}},i_{s^{\prime}+1},\ldots,\ldots i_{r-1},i_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{s^{\prime}},i_{s^{\prime}+1},\ldots,\ldots i_{r-1},i_{r})</annotation></semantics></math> both contain the directed
edge <math id="S5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s^{\prime}}\rightarrow i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s^{\prime}}\rightarrow i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and, in analogy to (<a href="#S5.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>) we
obtain the inequality</span></p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{s},i_{r})+w(i_{r},i_{s}^{\prime})\leq-2-w(i_{s^{\prime}},i_{s})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{s},i_{r})+w(i_{r},i_{s}^{\prime})\leq-2-w(i_{s^{\prime}},i_{s}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">Once again, <math id="S5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{s},i_{r}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{s},i_{r}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are diagonals, hence the
left hand side of (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>) is
<math id="S5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{s},i_{r})+\beta(i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{s},i_{r})+\beta(i_{r},i_{s^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> whereas the right hand side is at
most <math id="S5.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2-\alpha(i_{s^{\prime}},i_{s})=-2+\beta(i_{s},i_{s}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2-\alpha(i_{s^{\prime}},i_{s})=-2+\beta(i_{s},i_{s}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Once again, we obtain
a contradiction with (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the truncated affine arrangement <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,b}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,b}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then a valid associated weighted digraph is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic if and only if
it contains no <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle of length at most four.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">As noted in Remark <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Remark 1.5. ‣ 1.2. Deformations of a graphical arrangement ‣ 1. Preliminaries ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>, without loss of generality we may
assume that <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq b</annotation></semantics></math> holds. By Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>, it suffices to
verify the validity of the inequality (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>).
We distinguish several cases depending on the relative order of
<math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math> holds then
<math id="S5.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)=\beta(j,k)=\beta(i,k)=b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)=\beta(j,k)=\beta(i,k)=b-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and Equation (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) is
equivalent to <math id="S5.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b-1\leq 2(b-1)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b-1\leq 2(b-1)+1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is equivalent to <math id="S5.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math> holds then <math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)=\beta(j,k)=\beta(i,k)=a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)=\beta(j,k)=\beta(i,k)=a-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and
Equation (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) is equivalent to <math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-1\leq 2(a-1)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-1\leq 2(a-1)+1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is
equivalent to <math id="S5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">In all other subcases at least one of <math id="S5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S5.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b-1</annotation></semantics></math>, the right hand side of (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) is at least <math id="S5.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a-1)+(b-1)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a-1)+(b-1)+1</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the left hand side of (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) is at most <math id="S5.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The
inequality <math id="S5.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b-1\leq(a-1)+(b-1)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b-1\leq(a-1)+(b-1)+1</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to <math id="S5.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p14.1" class="ltx_p">It may be possible to extend Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5.5. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> to further cases, but
this can not be done by inspecting the value of <math id="S5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> alone. The next two examples
illustrate this fact and indicate some of the
difficulties in discussing the truncated affine arrangements which do
not fall under the validity of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5.5. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.6</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the case when <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=4</annotation></semantics></math> there is a valid
weighted digraph associated to the truncated affine arrangement
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{-1,4}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>4</mn><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{-1,4}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> whose shortest
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle has length <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. An
example is shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. This weighted digraph has <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>
vertices and for each <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math> we have either <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=b-1=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=b-1=3</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,i)=-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,i)=-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, or we have <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=a-1=-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=a-1=-2</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,i)=-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,i)=-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, only one of the arrows
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\rightarrow i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\rightarrow i</annotation></semantics></math> is present, the digraph is
a tournament. The weight of the directed cycle <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=(1,3,5,4,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=(1,3,5,4,2)</annotation></semantics></math>
is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\cdot 3+3\cdot(-2)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\cdot 3+3\cdot(-2)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, this cycle is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending. To show
that all other directed cycles have negative weight,
consider the following “potential function” <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> on the vertices:
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(1)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(2)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(2)=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(3)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(3)=3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(4)=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(4)=4</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(5)=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(5)=6</annotation></semantics></math>. The values
of this function are the circled numbers in
Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. For any arrow <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> that belongs to
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=\phi(j)-\phi(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=\phi(j)-\phi(i)</annotation></semantics></math> and for all other arrows <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)&lt;\phi(j)-\phi(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)&lt;\phi(j)-\phi(i)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence any cycle containing at
least one directed edge that does not belong to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> must have
negative weight.</span></p>
