<div id="S5.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.7</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the case when <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=3</annotation></semantics></math>, the truncated affine
arrangement <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{-1,3}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{-1,3}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the
hyperplanes <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=2</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. A
dilation by a factor of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2</annotation></semantics></math> puts the regions of
this hyperplane arrangement in bijection with the regions of
the Linial arrangement <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{0,2}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{0,2}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to which
Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5.5. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>, as well as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, Theorem
8.6]</cite>, are applicable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Looking at the formulas for the number of regions and the characteristic
polynomial <math id="S5.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{ab}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{ab}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>,
counting regions in a combinatorial way promises to be easier in the
cases when <math id="S5.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|a-b|\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|a-b|\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+b\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+b\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> hold for the parameters <math id="S5.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. As we will see in Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem11" title="Proposition 5.11. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a> below, these
are exactly the cases when the truncated
affine arrangements is separated and satisfies the weak triangle
inequality (as defined in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Separated deformations and the weak triangle inequality ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). As a direct
consequence of the definitions we obtain:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.8</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A contiguous integral deformation of the braid arrangement in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
is separated if and only if <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\alpha(i,j),\beta(i,j)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha(i,j),\beta(i,j)]</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.9</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The truncated affine arrangement <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{ab}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{ab}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a+b\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a+b\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> is separated if and only if <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> holds.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Next we characterize the cases when the weak triangle inequality is
satisfied by a separated contiguous integral deformation of the braid
arrangement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.10</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a separated contiguous integral deformation of the braid
arrangement in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This
satisfies the weak triangle inequality if and only if</span></p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)\leq\beta(i,k)+1\quad\mbox{and}\quad\beta(i,j)\leq\beta(k,j)+1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)\leq\beta(i,k)+1\quad\mbox{and}\quad\beta(i,j)\leq\beta(k,j)+1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">hold for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j,k\}\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots,n\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j,k\}\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots,n\}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume first that (<a href="#S5.E5" title="In Theorem 5.10. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>) is satisfied and let us compare
<math id="S5.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)+w(j,k)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)+w(j,k)+1</annotation></semantics></math>, where we assume that the weights
<math id="S5.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> are nonnegative. If the reverse arrows
<math id="S5.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> (of weight <math id="S5.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1-w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1-w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S5.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leftarrow k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leftarrow k</annotation></semantics></math> (of weight
<math id="S5.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1-w(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1-w(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math>) both exist, then the fact that the cycle <math id="S5.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,k,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,k,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S5.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic implies</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1-w(i,j)-1-w(j,k)+w(i,k)\leq-1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1-w(i,j)-1-w(j,k)+w(i,k)\leq-1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p17.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p17.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is equivalent to the weak triangle inequality</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)\leq w(i,j)+w(j,k)+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)\leq w(i,j)+w(j,k)+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p17.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p17.3.1" class="ltx_text">We are left to consider the cases when at least one of the arrows
<math id="S5.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leftarrow k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leftarrow k</annotation></semantics></math> does not exist. In this case either
<math id="S5.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=\beta(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=\beta(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,k)=\beta(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,k)=\beta(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math>. In either case the sum
<math id="S5.p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)+w(j,k)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)+w(j,k)+1</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="S5.p17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min(\beta(i,j),\beta(j,k))+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min(\beta(i,j),\beta(j,k))+1</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas
<math id="S5.p17.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S5.p17.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math>. The weak triangle inequality follows
from (<a href="#S5.E5" title="In Theorem 5.10. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next we prove the contrapositive of the converse. Assume
that (<a href="#S5.E5" title="In Theorem 5.10. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>) fails for some <math id="S5.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j,k\}</annotation></semantics></math>, without loss of
generality we may assume that <math id="S5.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)&gt;\beta(i,k)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)&gt;\beta(i,k)+1</annotation></semantics></math> holds. We
construct a valid associated weighted digraph violating the weak triangle
inequality as follows.We set <math id="S5.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=\beta(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=\beta(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> (hence there is no
arrow <math id="S5.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S5.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)=\beta(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)=\beta(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> (hence there is no
arrow <math id="S5.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow k</annotation></semantics></math>). We set <math id="S5.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,k)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,k)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(k,j)=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(k,j)=-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that
there is no <math id="S5.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle on the restriction of our weighted digraph to
<math id="S5.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j,k\}</annotation></semantics></math> as the only cycle is <math id="S5.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math>, which has weight <math id="S5.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. We
fix a linear order on <math id="S5.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> in such a way that <math id="S5.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> are the smallest vertices in this order. For any pair of vertices
<math id="S5.p18.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i^{\prime},j^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i^{\prime},j^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> not
contained in <math id="S5.p18.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j,k\}</annotation></semantics></math> we set <math id="S5.p18.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i^{\prime},j^{\prime})=\beta(i^{\prime},j^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i^{\prime},j^{\prime})=\beta(i^{\prime},j^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p18.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the smaller vertex in our order. This choice guarantees that there is no
arrow <math id="S5.p18.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}\leftarrow j^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}\leftarrow j^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence our weighted digraph does not contain any other
cycle than <math id="S5.p18.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.11</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume the truncated affine arrangement <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{ab}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{ab}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfies <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a\leq b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. This integral and separated arrangement
satisfies the weak triangle inequality if and only of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\leq a+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\leq a+1</annotation></semantics></math> holds.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">The arrangement is separated by Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem9" title="Corollary 5.9. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a>. Assume first
that <math id="S5.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\leq a+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\leq a+1</annotation></semantics></math> holds. For all <math id="S5.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j,k\}</annotation></semantics></math> the value of <math id="S5.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is
at most <math id="S5.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b-1</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the value of <math id="S5.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> and of <math id="S5.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(k,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(k,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is at
least <math id="S5.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The inequality (<a href="#S5.E5" title="In Theorem 5.10. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>) is a direct consequence of
<math id="S5.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\leq a+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\leq a+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, if <math id="S5.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq b-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq b-2</annotation></semantics></math> holds, consider <math id="S5.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S5.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)=\beta(2,3)=b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)=\beta(2,3)=b-1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,k)=\beta(2,1)=a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,k)=\beta(2,1)=a-1</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(2,3)\leq\beta(2,1)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(2,3)\leq\beta(2,1)+1</annotation></semantics></math> fails as <math id="S5.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b-1\not\leq(a-1)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≰</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b-1\not\leq(a-1)+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.12</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the truncated affine arrangement <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{13}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>13</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{13}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and the
associated <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraph given by <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(2,1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(2,1)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(1,3)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(1,3)=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(3,1)=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(3,1)=-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(2,3)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(2,3)=2</annotation></semantics></math>. The arrangement is saturated, the total order
of gains is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=213" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>213</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=213</annotation></semantics></math>. Not only the weak triangle inequality fails
because of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(2,3)&gt;w(2,1)+w(1,3)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(2,3)&gt;w(2,1)+w(1,3)+1</annotation></semantics></math>, but the largest weight path form
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)=2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(2)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(2)=1</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\rightarrow 3\rightarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\rightarrow 3\rightarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>, which
contains an edge of negative weight.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p21.1" class="ltx_p">For truncated affine arrangements, a combinatorial approach to the case
<math id="S5.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a\leq b\leq a+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a\leq b\leq a+1</annotation></semantics></math> is further facilitated by the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.13</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a truncated affine arrangement <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{ab}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{ab}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfying <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a\leq b\leq a+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a\leq b\leq a+1</annotation></semantics></math>. A valid associated weighted digraph is
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic if and only if in contains no <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle of length three.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">As a consequence of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5.5. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>, it suffices
to show that there is no <math id="S5.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab</annotation></semantics></math>-weighted digraph in which a shortest
<math id="S5.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle of length <math id="S5.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> would exist.
Assume by contradiction that <math id="S5.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{0},i_{1},i_{2},i_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{0},i_{1},i_{2},i_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> is such a
cycle. By Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, for each diagonal pair
<math id="S5.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{r},i_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{r},i_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, only one of the directed edges <math id="S5.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}\rightarrow i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}\rightarrow i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}\leftarrow i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}\leftarrow i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is present. After cyclic rotation of the labels,
if necessary, we may assume that <math id="S5.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}\rightarrow i_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}\rightarrow i_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}\rightarrow i_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}\rightarrow i_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are the directed edges present in our weighted digraph. By our
assumption, the directed cycles <math id="S5.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{0},i_{2},i_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{0},i_{2},i_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{1},i_{3},i_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{1},i_{3},i_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> are
not <math id="S5.p22.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic, we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{0},i_{2},i_{3})\leq-1\quad\mbox{and}\quad w(i_{1},i_{3},i_{0})\leq-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{0},i_{2},i_{3})\leq-1\quad\mbox{and}\quad w(i_{1},i_{3},i_{0})\leq-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p22.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p22.2.1" class="ltx_text">In analogy to the derivation of (<a href="#S5.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>), adding <math id="S5.p22.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{1},i_{2})-w(i_{3},i_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{1},i_{2})-w(i_{3},i_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
on both sides of the sum of the two inequalities yields</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{0},i_{2})+w(i_{1},i_{3})+w(i_{0},i_{1},i_{2},i_{3})\leq-2+w(i_{1},i_{2})-w(i_{3},i_{0})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{0},i_{2})+w(i_{1},i_{3})+w(i_{0},i_{1},i_{2},i_{3})\leq-2+w(i_{1},i_{2})-w(i_{3},i_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p22.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p22.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p22.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{0},i_{1},i_{2},i_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{0},i_{1},i_{2},i_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p22.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending, the last inequality implies</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{0},i_{2})+w(i_{1},i_{3})\leq-2+w(i_{1},i_{2})-w(i_{3},i_{0})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{0},i_{2})+w(i_{1},i_{3})\leq-2+w(i_{1},i_{2})-w(i_{3},i_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p22.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p22.4.1" class="ltx_text">The left hand side is at least <math id="S5.p22.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(a-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(a-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the right hand side is at most
<math id="S5.p22.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2+(b-1)+(b-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2+(b-1)+(b-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(a-1)\leq-2+(b-1)+(b-1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(a-1)\leq-2+(b-1)+(b-1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p22.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p22.5.1" class="ltx_text">which is equivalent to <math id="S5.p22.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq b-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Together with <math id="S5.p22.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq b-1</annotation></semantics></math> we obtain
<math id="S5.p22.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=b-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=b-1</annotation></semantics></math> and all inequalities used must be equalities. But this implies
<math id="S5.p22.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}&gt;i_{2}&gt;i_{1}&gt;i_{3}&gt;i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}&gt;i_{2}&gt;i_{1}&gt;i_{3}&gt;i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 5.13. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a> may be extended to contiguous integral
deformations of the braid arrangement using the following auxiliary
terminology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.14</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A separated contiguous integral deformation of the braid arrangement in
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the
weak triangle inequality has a crossing <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-pattern if there is a
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-element subset <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{1},i_{2},j_{1},j_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{1},i_{2},j_{1},j_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{1},j_{1})&lt;\beta(i_{1},j_{2})\quad\mbox{and}\quad\beta(i_{2},j_{1})&gt;\beta(i_{2},j_{2})\quad\mbox{hold}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">hold</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{1},j_{1})&lt;\beta(i_{1},j_{2})\quad\mbox{and}\quad\beta(i_{2},j_{1})&gt;\beta(i_{2},j_{2})\quad\mbox{hold}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.15</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be a separated contiguous integral deformation of
the braid arrangement in
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the weak triangle inequality. Then <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>
has a valid associated weighted digraph containing a minimal
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle of length <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, if and only if <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> has a
crossing <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-pattern.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume first that <math id="S5.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> has a crossing <math id="S5.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-pattern, that is,
a set <math id="S5.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{1},i_{2},j_{2},j_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{1},i_{2},j_{2},j_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying (<a href="#S5.E6" title="In Definition 5.14. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>). Introducing
<math id="S5.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\beta(i_{1},j_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\beta(i_{1},j_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, as a consequence of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 5.10. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>
and (<a href="#S5.E6" title="In Definition 5.14. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>) we obtain <math id="S5.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{1},j_{2})=\beta+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{1},j_{2})=\beta+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly,
introducing <math id="S5.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}=\beta(i_{2},j_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}=\beta(i_{2},j_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 5.10. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>
and (<a href="#S5.E6" title="In Definition 5.14. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>) yield
<math id="S5.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{1},j_{2})=\beta^{\prime}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{1},j_{2})=\beta^{\prime}+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 5.10. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a> also implies
<math id="S5.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{1},j_{2})-\beta(i_{2},j_{2})\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{1},j_{2})-\beta(i_{2},j_{2})\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S5.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta+1-\beta^{\prime}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta+1-\beta^{\prime}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.p24.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{2},j_{1})-\beta(i_{1},j_{1})\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{2},j_{1})-\beta(i_{1},j_{1})\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, that is,
<math id="S5.p24.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}+1-\beta\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}+1-\beta\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. We obtain that <math id="S5.p24.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta-\beta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta-\beta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p24.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}-\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}-\beta</annotation></semantics></math>
are both at most zero, hence <math id="S5.p24.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> must equal <math id="S5.p24.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
