<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>A valid weighted digraph with a minimal <math id="S5.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending <math id="S5.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the weighted digraph shown in
Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Proof. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The weights <math id="S5.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{1},j_{1})=\beta(i_{1},j_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{1},j_{1})=\beta(i_{1},j_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{2},j_{2})=\beta(i_{2},j_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{2},j_{2})=\beta(i_{2},j_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{2},j_{1})=\beta(i_{2},j_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{2},j_{1})=\beta(i_{2},j_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> are largest
possible, hence there is no arrow <math id="S5.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}\leftarrow j_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}\leftarrow j_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{2}\leftarrow j_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{2}\leftarrow j_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{2}\leftarrow j_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{2}\leftarrow j_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The weight <math id="S5.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{1},j_{1})=\beta(i_{1},j_{1})-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{1},j_{1})=\beta(i_{1},j_{1})-1</annotation></semantics></math> is
less than <math id="S5.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{1},j_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{1},j_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> hence there is also an arrow <math id="S5.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}\leftarrow j_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}\leftarrow j_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S5.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j_{1},i_{1})=-\beta-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j_{1},i_{1})=-\beta-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The choices <math id="S5.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{1},i_{2})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{1},i_{2})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p25.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j_{1},i_{2})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j_{1},i_{2})=0</annotation></semantics></math> are valid since the arrangement is separated, (dashed)
the reverse arrows <math id="S5.p25.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}\rightarrow i_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}\rightarrow i_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively <math id="S5.p25.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1}\rightarrow j_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1}\rightarrow j_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are only present if <math id="S5.p25.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{1},i_{2})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{1},i_{2})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively
<math id="S5.p25.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(j_{1},j_{2})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(j_{1},j_{2})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> hold. The weight of each directed cycle of length <math id="S5.p25.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>
is <math id="S5.p25.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)</annotation></semantics></math> (as it should), and the same holds for the <math id="S5.p25.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles
<math id="S5.p25.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{1},j_{1},j_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{1},j_{1},j_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p25.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{1},i_{2},j_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{1},i_{2},j_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S5.p25.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle <math id="S5.p25.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{1},i_{2},j_{1},j_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{1},i_{2},j_{1},j_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>
has weight zero. Just like in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 5.10. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>, we may
select a linear order on <math id="S5.p25.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> in such a way that
<math id="S5.p25.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1},i_{2},j_{1},j_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1},i_{2},j_{1},j_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are the least elements, and for any pair of
vertices <math id="S5.p25.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i^{\prime},j^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i^{\prime},j^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> not contained in <math id="S5.p25.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i_{1},i_{2},j_{1},j_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{1},i_{2},j_{1},j_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> we set
<math id="S5.p25.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i^{\prime},j^{\prime})=\beta(i^{\prime},j^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i^{\prime},j^{\prime})=\beta(i^{\prime},j^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p25.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the smaller vertex in our order.
The resulting weighted digraph is valid, and its only <math id="S5.p25.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending
cycle is <math id="S5.p25.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{1},i_{2},j_{1},j_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{1},i_{2},j_{1},j_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume next that a valid weighted digraph associated to <math id="S5.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>
has a minimal <math id="S5.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle <math id="S5.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{0},i_{1},i_{2},i_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{0},i_{1},i_{2},i_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> of length four. We will
adapt the proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 5.13. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a> to tackle this case. As
before, we may use
Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 5.3. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, and after a cyclic rotation we may assume
that <math id="S5.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}\rightarrow i_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}\rightarrow i_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}\rightarrow i_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}\rightarrow i_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are the diagonals of finite weight present. Using the
fact that the directed cycles <math id="S5.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{0},i_{2},i_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{0},i_{2},i_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{1},i_{3},i_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{1},i_{3},i_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> are
not <math id="S5.p26.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic, the same calculation yields</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{0},i_{2})+w(i_{1},i_{3})\leq-2+w(i_{1},i_{2})-w(i_{3},i_{0})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{0},i_{2})+w(i_{1},i_{3})\leq-2+w(i_{1},i_{2})-w(i_{3},i_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p26.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p26.2.1" class="ltx_text">The left hand side is exactly <math id="S5.p26.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{0},i_{2})+\beta(i_{1},i_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{0},i_{2})+\beta(i_{1},i_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>, as there
is no arrow <math id="S5.p26.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}\rightarrow i_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}\rightarrow i_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p26.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}\rightarrow i_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}\rightarrow i_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
right hand side is at most
<math id="S5.p26.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2+\beta(i_{1},i_{2})-\alpha(i_{3},i_{0})=-2+\beta(i_{1},i_{2})+\beta(i_{0},i_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2+\beta(i_{1},i_{2})-\alpha(i_{3},i_{0})=-2+\beta(i_{1},i_{2})+\beta(i_{0},i_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. We
obtain the inequality</span></p>
<table id="S5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{0},i_{2})+\beta(i_{1},i_{3})\leq-2+\beta(i_{1},i_{2})+\beta(i_{0},i_{3})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{0},i_{2})+\beta(i_{1},i_{3})\leq-2+\beta(i_{1},i_{2})+\beta(i_{0},i_{3}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p26.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p26.3.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 5.10. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>, we have <math id="S5.p26.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{1},i_{2})\leq\beta(i_{1},i_{3})+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{1},i_{2})\leq\beta(i_{1},i_{3})+1</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.p26.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{0},i_{3})\leq\beta(i_{0},i_{2})+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{0},i_{3})\leq\beta(i_{0},i_{2})+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Using these as upper bounds
for the terms on the right hand side of (<a href="#S5.E7" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>) we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{0},i_{2})+\beta(i_{1},i_{3})\leq-2+\beta(i_{1},i_{2})+\beta(i_{0},i_{3})\leq\beta(i_{0},i_{2})+\beta(i_{1},i_{3})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{0},i_{2})+\beta(i_{1},i_{3})\leq-2+\beta(i_{1},i_{2})+\beta(i_{0},i_{3})\leq\beta(i_{0},i_{2})+\beta(i_{1},i_{3}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p26.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p26.4.1" class="ltx_text">All inequalities must be equalities, in particular we get
<math id="S5.p26.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{1},i_{2})=\beta(i_{1},i_{3})+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{1},i_{2})=\beta(i_{1},i_{3})+1</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p26.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{0},i_{3})=\beta(i_{0},i_{2})+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{0},i_{3})=\beta(i_{0},i_{2})+1</annotation></semantics></math>. As a consequence
<math id="S5.p26.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{1},i_{2})&gt;\beta(i_{1},i_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{1},i_{2})&gt;\beta(i_{1},i_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p26.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i_{0},i_{3})&lt;\beta(i_{0},i_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i_{0},i_{3})&lt;\beta(i_{0},i_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>
hold, that is, we have a crossing <math id="S5.p26.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-pattern.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Extended Shi arrangements</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we show how labeled weighted digraphs may be used to fill in
the omitted details in Stanley’s proof of the injectivity of the <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pak-Stanley labeling</span> of the regions of the extended Shi arrangement
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, 2.1 Theorem]</cite>. We also revisit and generalize the
Athanasiadis-Linusson labeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> of its
regions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem9" title="Corollary 5.9. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a> the extended Shi arrangement is separated:
for each region there is a unique permutation
<math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1)\sigma(2)\ldots\sigma(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1)\sigma(2)\ldots\sigma(n)</annotation></semantics></math> such
that <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\sigma(i),\sigma(j))\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\sigma(i),\sigma(j))\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently,
every region that is represented by a weighted digraph whose total order of
gains is <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of the cone
<math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma(1)}&gt;x_{\sigma(2)}&gt;\cdots&gt;x_{\sigma(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma(1)}&gt;x_{\sigma(2)}&gt;\cdots&gt;x_{\sigma(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>. For
<math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j</annotation></semantics></math>, there is always a
directed edge <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math>, and there is no arrow
<math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> exactly when <math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is as large as possible, that is,
<math id="S6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=\beta(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=\beta(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> where</p>
<table id="S6.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)=\begin{cases}a&amp;\mbox{when $i&lt;j$},\\
a-1&amp;\mbox{when $i&gt;j$}.\\
\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>a</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>when </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>when </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)=\begin{cases}a&amp;\mbox{when $i&lt;j$},\\
a-1&amp;\mbox{when $i&gt;j$}.\\
\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">By Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 5.13. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a> we may verify
the <math id="S6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic condition in a valid associated weighted digraph by
only checking the directed cycles of length three. Consider a set
<math id="S6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j,k\}\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j,k\}\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, without loss of generality we may assume
<math id="S6.p2.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k</annotation></semantics></math>. There is no <math id="S6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle
<math id="S6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j,k)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if either there is no arrow <math id="S6.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow k</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S6.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)+w(j,k)-1-w(i,k)\leq-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)+w(j,k)-1-w(i,k)\leq-1</annotation></semantics></math> holds. This condition may be
equivalently rewritten as</p>
<table id="S6.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w(i,k)\geq\min(\beta(i,j),w(i,j)+w(j,k))\quad\mbox{for
$i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)\geq\min(\beta(i,j),w(i,j)+w(j,k))\quad\mbox{for
$i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">There is no <math id="S6.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle <math id="S6.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,k,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,k,j)</annotation></semantics></math> exactly when either one of the
arrows <math id="S6.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\rightarrow i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\rightarrow i</annotation></semantics></math> is missing, or we have
<math id="S6.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)-1-w(i,j)-1-w(j,k)\leq-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)-1-w(i,j)-1-w(j,k)\leq-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The last inequality is equivalent
to</p>
<table id="S6.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w(i,k)\leq w(i,j)+w(j,k)+1\quad\mbox{for
$i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)\leq w(i,j)+w(j,k)+1\quad\mbox{for
$i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Note that (<a href="#S6.E2" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>) also holds when one of the
arrows <math id="S6.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\rightarrow i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\rightarrow i</annotation></semantics></math> is missing, as <math id="S6.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,k)\leq\beta(i,j)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,k)\leq\beta(i,j)+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,k)\leq\beta(j,k)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,k)\leq\beta(j,k)+1</annotation></semantics></math> always hold, regardless
of the relative order of the numbers <math id="S6.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. To summarize,
given a valid associated digraph satisfying
<math id="S6.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p2.m31" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j</annotation></semantics></math>, the weighted digraph is
<math id="S6.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic if and only if (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S6.E2" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>) hold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the <span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pak-Stanley labeling</span> associates to each region <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>
a vector <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(R)=(f(1),f(2),\ldots,f(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(R)=(f(1),f(2),\ldots,f(n))</annotation></semantics></math> in the following way:</p>
<ol id="S6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The label of the central region <math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}&gt;x_{2}&gt;\cdots&gt;x_{n}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}&gt;x_{2}&gt;\cdots&gt;x_{n}-1</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0,\ldots,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0,\ldots,0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is an already labeled region, and the region <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
separated from <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> by the unique hyperplane <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=m</annotation></semantics></math> for some
<math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(R^{\prime})=\lambda(R)+e_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(R^{\prime})=\lambda(R)+e_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(R^{\prime})=\lambda(R)+e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(R^{\prime})=\lambda(R)+e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are the
standard basis vectors of <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> Stanley gives a detailed equivalent definition of
the function <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)</annotation></semantics></math> in the case when <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We now extend this
equivalent definition to all extended Shi arrangements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a valid <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic associated weighted digraph of
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,a+1}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,a+1}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with weight function <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the Pak-Stanley label <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f(1),\ldots,f(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f(1),\ldots,f(n))</annotation></semantics></math> of the corresponding region as</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f(i)=\sum_{i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j}w(i,j)+|\{(i,j)\&gt;:\&gt;i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j\mbox{ and }i&gt;j\}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">+</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.498em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> and </mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)=\sum_{i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j}w(i,j)+|\{(i,j)\&gt;:\&gt;i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j\mbox{ and }i&gt;j\}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We let the reader verify that the two definitions are equivalent. For
example, in the case when <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq w\leq a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq w\leq a-1</annotation></semantics></math> hold,
<math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=w</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to stating <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;w+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w&lt;x_{i}-x_{j}&lt;w+1</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is
the number of hyperplanes of the form <math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=m</annotation></semantics></math> crossed while reaching
our region from the central region.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> we call the sum <math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum_{i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j}w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j}w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> the number of <span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">separations</span>, whereas <math id="S6.p4.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|\{(i,j)\&gt;:\&gt;i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j\mbox{ and }i&gt;j\}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.498em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi><mtext> and </mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\{(i,j)\&gt;:\&gt;i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j\mbox{ and }i&gt;j\}|</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of <span id="S6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inversions</span>. We do not need to prove the equivalence of the above
definitions, as we may easily show the following statement
directly. Recall that an <math id="S6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-parking function</span> is a sequence
<math id="S6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f(1),\ldots,f(n))\in\mathbb{N}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f(1),\ldots,f(n))\in\mathbb{N}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> whose monotonic rearrangement
<math id="S6.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}(1)\leq\tilde{f}(2)\leq\cdots\leq\tilde{f}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}(1)\leq\tilde{f}(2)\leq\cdots\leq\tilde{f}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies
<math id="S6.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\tilde{f}(i)\leq a(i-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\tilde{f}(i)\leq a(i-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Pak-Stanley labels <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f(1),\ldots,f(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f(1),\ldots,f(n))</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-parking functions.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is a direct consequence of the fact that, for each <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j</annotation></semantics></math>, the number <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is not an inversion
and at most <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-1</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is an inversion. Hence the total number of
inversions and separations contributing to <math id="S6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> times
the number of labels <math id="S6.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> succeeding <math id="S6.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Next we rephrase Stanley’s key lemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, p. 363]</cite> to
match our terminology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> holds then we
have <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)&gt;f(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)&gt;f(j)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume first <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math> holds, i.e., <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is an inversion. Then for each
inversion <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math> the pair <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is also inversion, hence we may
identify the set of all inversions of <math id="S6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> with those inversions <math id="S6.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S6.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfy the stronger inequality <math id="S6.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>. Regarding
separations, note that (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>) implies <math id="S6.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)\geq w(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)\geq w(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math> in
almost all cases: either we have <math id="S6.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)\geq w(i,j)+w(j,k)\geq w(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)\geq w(i,j)+w(j,k)\geq w(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math>
or we have <math id="S6.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)\geq\beta(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)\geq\beta(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> forcing <math id="S6.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)\geq a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)\geq a-1</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,k)\leq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,k)\leq a</annotation></semantics></math>. The inequality <math id="S6.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)&lt;w(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)&lt;w(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math> can only hold when we have
<math id="S6.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;k&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;k&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,k)=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,k)=a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)=a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)=a-1</annotation></semantics></math>. In this exceptional case <math id="S6.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math>
contributes one less to the separations of <math id="S6.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> than <math id="S6.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math> to the
separations of <math id="S6.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, but this lag is offset by the presence of the
inversion <math id="S6.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> which has no corresponding inversion <math id="S6.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence we
have <math id="S6.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)\geq f(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)\geq f(j)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the presence of the additional inversion
<math id="S6.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> (contributing to <math id="S6.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)</annotation></semantics></math> only) makes the inequality strict.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume next <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> holds. Once again, we may use (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>) to
state <math id="S6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)\geq w(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)\geq w(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math> almost always. In the
exceptional case <math id="S6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;k&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;k&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,k)=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,k)=a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)=a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)=a-1</annotation></semantics></math> must hold, and
<math id="S6.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is an additional inversion. Regarding an inversion <math id="S6.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math>,
we may match it to <math id="S6.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is an inversion, the unmatched
inversions <math id="S6.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="S6.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;k&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;k&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>. For these (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>) implies</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)\geq\min(a,w(i,j)+w(j,k))&gt;w(j,k)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)\geq\min(a,w(i,j)+w(j,k))&gt;w(j,k),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">as <math id="S6.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,k)\leq a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,k)\leq a-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> hold, hence <math id="S6.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> contributes more
separations to <math id="S6.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)</annotation></semantics></math> than <math id="S6.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(j)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore we have
<math id="S6.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)\geq f(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)\geq f(j)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the presence of the additional separations
contributed by <math id="S6.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)</annotation></semantics></math> makes the inequality strict.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Now we are ready to fill in the omitted details in the proof
of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, 2.1 Theorem]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Stanley)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The labels of the regions of the extended Shi arrangement are the
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-parking functions of length <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, each occurring exactly once.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">The fact that the labels are <math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-parking functions have been shown in
Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 6.2. ‣ 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> above. As in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, 2.1
Theorem]</cite>, we will only show the injectivity of the
labeling, and then we will rely on existing results in the literature to
confirm that the number of regions equals the number of <math id="S6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-parking
functions.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given an <math id="S6.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-parking function <math id="S6.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f(1),\ldots,f(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f(1),\ldots,f(n))</annotation></semantics></math>, we insert the labels
<math id="S6.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S6.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> one by one and show the uniqueness of the place and of the
function values <math id="S6.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> one step at a time. As in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, 2.1
Theorem]</cite>, we insert the labels <math id="S6.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in increasing order
of the value <math id="S6.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)</annotation></semantics></math>, and we insert labels with the same <math id="S6.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-value
in decreasing order of their numerical value. Suppose <math id="S6.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>
is the most recently inserted label. If this label is preceded by any
label <math id="S6.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, then by Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 6.3. ‣ 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> we must have
<math id="S6.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i^{\prime},i)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i^{\prime},i)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>, otherwise we get <math id="S6.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i^{\prime})&gt;f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i^{\prime})&gt;f(i)</annotation></semantics></math> in contradiction
with our defined order of insertion. Note that we must also have
<math id="S6.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i^{\prime})=f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i^{\prime})=f(i)</annotation></semantics></math> in this case. We also obtained that the label <math id="S6.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> can
not contribute any inversion or separation to any preceding <math id="S6.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. This
guarantees that at the insertion of <math id="S6.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> we only need to make sure that
the instances of (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>) and  (<a href="#S6.E2" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>) involving <math id="S6.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>
are satisfied and that the values <math id="S6.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> for all previously inserted <math id="S6.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>
succeeding <math id="S6.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> are consistently defined.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">First we show that <math id="S6.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> can not be inserted at two different places into
<math id="S6.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that the word representing the currently inserted
labels is <math id="S6.p12.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\sigma}=i_{1}\cdots i_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\sigma}=i_{1}\cdots i_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> could be inserted
either right
after <math id="S6.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> or right after <math id="S6.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S6.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;s</annotation></semantics></math> (we set <math id="S6.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is
inserted as the first letter). Let <math id="S6.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively <math id="S6.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> be a
weight function arising with the insertion of <math id="S6.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> right after <math id="S6.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>,
respectively <math id="S6.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. As in the proof of Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 6.3. ‣ 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>,
 (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>) implies <math id="S6.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{r}(i,k)\geq w_{r}(i_{s},k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{r}(i,k)\geq w_{r}(i_{s},k)</annotation></semantics></math>
for almost all <math id="S6.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S6.p12.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{s}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k</annotation></semantics></math>, the conclusion
<math id="S6.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{r}(i,k)\geq w_{s}(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{r}(i,k)\geq w_{s}(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> fails to hold only
when we have <math id="S6.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{r}(i_{s},k)=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{r}(i_{s},k)=a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{r}(i,k)=a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{r}(i,k)=a-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}&lt;k&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}&lt;k&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the other hand,  (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>) implies</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}(i_{s},k)\geq w_{s}(i_{s},i)+w_{s}(i,k)\geq w_{s}(i,k)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}(i_{s},k)\geq w_{s}(i_{s},i)+w_{s}(i,k)\geq w_{s}(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">for all <math id="S6.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S6.p12.m23" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{s}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k</annotation></semantics></math>: the exceptional case
<math id="S6.p12.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}(i_{s},k)=a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}(i_{s},k)=a-1</annotation></semantics></math> , <math id="S6.p12.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}(i,k)=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}(i,k)=a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p12.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;k&lt;i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;k&lt;i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot occur, as it would
create the inversion <math id="S6.p12.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i_{s},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i_{s},i)</annotation></semantics></math>, in contradiction with the possibility of
inserting <math id="S6.p12.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> right after <math id="S6.p12.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining the last two observations and
using the fact that <math id="S6.p12.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{r}(i_{s},k)=w_{s}(i_{s},k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{r}(i_{s},k)=w_{s}(i_{s},k)</annotation></semantics></math> (it is the same weight
function created before the insertion of <math id="S6.p12.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>), we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S6.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{r}(i,k)\geq w_{s}(i,k)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{r}(i,k)\geq w_{s}(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p12.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p12.3.1" class="ltx_text">for almost all <math id="S6.p12.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S6.p12.m33" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{s}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k</annotation></semantics></math>. We only need to
check the case when <math id="S6.p12.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{r}(i_{s},k)=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{r}(i_{s},k)=a</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p12.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{r}(i,k)=a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{r}(i,k)=a-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p12.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}&lt;k&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}&lt;k&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>
hold. In this exceptional case <math id="S6.p12.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math> implies that the maximum value of
<math id="S6.p12.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p12.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-1=w_{r}(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-1=w_{r}(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math>, hence (<a href="#S6.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>) holds in this exceptional
case as well. As a consequence the total number of separations of <math id="S6.p12.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>
can not be greater when we insert it right after <math id="S6.p12.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> than in the case when
we insert it right after <math id="S6.p12.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. The same holds for the number of inversions,
finally the inversion <math id="S6.p12.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,i_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,i_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> is only present when we insert <math id="S6.p12.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> right
after <math id="S6.p12.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. In conclusion we can not obtain the same value <math id="S6.p12.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)</annotation></semantics></math> in
both scenarios.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">We are left to show that inserting <math id="S6.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> at the same place can not be
continued by extending the weight function in two different ways. By
Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 6.3. ‣ 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a> we must set <math id="S6.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i^{\prime},i)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i^{\prime},i)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all previously
inserted <math id="S6.p13.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i^{\prime}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i</annotation></semantics></math>, variation may only occur in the
definition of <math id="S6.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S6.p13.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume there are
two different extensions: <math id="S6.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of the weight function. Then
there is a leftmost <math id="S6.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in the order <math id="S6.p13.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> for which
<math id="S6.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}(i,j)\neq w_{2}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}(i,j)\neq w_{2}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>, without loss of generality we may assume
<math id="S6.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}(i,j)&lt;w_{2}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}(i,j)&lt;w_{2}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition, for all <math id="S6.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
<math id="S6.p13.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S6.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}(i,k)=w_{2}(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}(i,k)=w_{2}(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math>, these
<math id="S6.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>’s contribute the same separation to <math id="S6.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)</annotation></semantics></math> in either case. On the
other hand, for all <math id="S6.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S6.p13.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k</annotation></semantics></math>, the
inequality (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>) implies</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}(i,k)\geq\min(\beta(i,k),w_{2}(i,j)+w_{2}(j,k))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}(i,k)\geq\min(\beta(i,k),w_{2}(i,j)+w_{2}(j,k)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">Using <math id="S6.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}(i,j)\geq w_{1}(i,j)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}(i,j)\geq w_{1}(i,j)+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}(j,k)=w_{1}(j,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}(j,k)=w_{1}(j,k)</annotation></semantics></math> (since <math id="S6.p13.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.p13.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> were inserted before <math id="S6.p13.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>) we may rewrite this as</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}(i,k)\geq\min(\beta(i,k),w_{1}(i,j)+w_{1}(j,k)+1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}(i,k)\geq\min(\beta(i,k),w_{1}(i,j)+w_{1}(j,k)+1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p13.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text">The inequality (<a href="#S6.E2" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>) implies</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}(i,k)\geq\min(\beta(i,k),w_{1}(i,k))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}(i,k)\geq\min(\beta(i,k),w_{1}(i,k)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p13.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p13.4.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S6.p13.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum value of <math id="S6.p13.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain
<math id="S6.p13.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}(i,k)\geq w_{1}(i,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}(i,k)\geq w_{1}(i,k)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p13.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
<math id="S6.p13.m28" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}k</annotation></semantics></math>. Keeping in mind <math id="S6.p13.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}(i,j)&lt;w_{2}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}(i,j)&lt;w_{2}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a
strictly larger separation in the computation of <math id="S6.p13.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)</annotation></semantics></math> when we use
<math id="S6.p13.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S6.p13.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The set of inversions being the same, we can not
obtain the same <math id="S6.p13.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(i)</annotation></semantics></math> using either extension of the weight function.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.5</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mazin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> has shown that the Pak-Stanley labeling of the
regions of the extended Shi arrangement is surjective. Together with
Stanley’s above result we have a self-contained proof of the fact that
the Pak-Stanley labeling is a bijection between the regions of the
regions of the extended Shi arrangement and the <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-parking functions.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p14.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude this section by revisiting and generalizing the construction
of Athanasiadis and Linusson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.6</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We say that the regions of a contiguous, separated and integral
deformation of the braid arrangement in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> given by the equations</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=m\quad m\in[-\beta(j,i),\beta(i,j)]\quad\mbox{for $1\leq i&lt;j&lt;n$}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for </mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=m\quad m\in[-\beta(j,i),\beta(i,j)]\quad\mbox{for $1\leq i&lt;j&lt;n$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">have Athanasiadis-Linusson diagrams if for each <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> the set
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta(i,j)\&gt;:\&gt;i\neq j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.498em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.498em">:</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta(i,j)\&gt;:\&gt;i\neq j\}</annotation></semantics></math> has at most two elements and these
elements are consecutive nonnegative integers. We set
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(j)=\min_{i\neq j}\beta(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(j)=\min_{i\neq j}\beta(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Note that for the extended Shi arrangement <math id="S6.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,a+1}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,a+1}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
we have <math id="S6.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(1)=\cdots=\beta(n)=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(1)=\cdots=\beta(n)=a</annotation></semantics></math>. More generally, as a consequence
of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 5.10. ‣ 5. Contiguous integral deformations ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.10</span></a>, the regions of every contiguous, separated and
integral deformation of the braid arrangement satisfying the weak triangle
inequality have Athanasiadis-Linusson diagrams.
When a contiguous, separated and integral deformation of the
braid arrangement in <math id="S6.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> has Athanasiadis-Linusson
diagrams, we define one for each of its regions essentially the same
way as it is done in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p16" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S6.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fix a representative <math id="S6.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{x}=(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{x}=(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> of the
region. These appear on the reversed number line in the order
<math id="S6.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\sigma(1)}&gt;x_{\sigma(2)}&gt;\cdots&gt;x_{\sigma(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\sigma(1)}&gt;x_{\sigma(2)}&gt;\cdots&gt;x_{\sigma(n)}</annotation></semantics></math>. (The arrangement
being separated forces <math id="S6.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> to be independent from the choice of
<math id="S6.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{x}</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(j)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(j)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we also
mark <math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}+\beta(j),x_{j}+\beta(j)-1,\ldots,x_{j}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}+\beta(j),x_{j}+\beta(j)-1,\ldots,x_{j}+1</annotation></semantics></math> on the reversed
number line and we draw an arc connecting <math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}+k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}+k+1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}+k</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,1,\ldots,\beta(j)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,1,\ldots,\beta(j)-1</annotation></semantics></math>. We label all of these points with <math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S6.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}\subseteq\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> we
also draw an arc between <math id="S6.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}+\beta(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}+\beta(j)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S6.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)=\beta(j)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)=\beta(j)+1</annotation></semantics></math>
<math id="S6.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}&gt;\beta(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}&gt;\beta(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> holds.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We remove all nested arcs, that is, all arcs that contain another arc.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S6.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p17.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of weighted digraphs, the permutation <math id="S6.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the total
order of gains, and in step (3) we add an arc between <math id="S6.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}+\beta(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}+\beta(j)</annotation></semantics></math> exactly when <math id="S6.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=\beta(i,j)=\beta(j)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=\beta(i,j)=\beta(j)+1</annotation></semantics></math> holds. In such a
case <math id="S6.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S6.p17.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j</annotation></semantics></math> regardless of the choice of
<math id="S6.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. In all other cases, assuming
<math id="S6.p17.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}j</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S6.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=w</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the point
representing <math id="S6.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is to the left of the point <math id="S6.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}+w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}+w</annotation></semantics></math>, but not to the
left of <math id="S6.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}+w+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}+w+1</annotation></semantics></math> (if <math id="S6.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w&lt;\beta(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w&lt;\beta(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> and there is a point representing
<math id="S6.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}+w+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}+w+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the valid <math id="S6.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic weighted digraphs bijectively
represent the regions of our hyperplane arrangement and they can be
uniquely reconstructed from the associated Athanasiadis-Linusson
diagrams, there is always a bijection between the regions and their
Athanasiadis-Linusson diagrams. It seems hard, however, to characterize
which diagrams may occur.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
