<div id="S6.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.8</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given an Athanasiadis-Linusson diagram, we define the parking tree
representing it as follows:</span></p>
<ol id="S6.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S6.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Replace the labels </span><math id="S6.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S6.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S6.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,…, </span><math id="S6.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{\beta(j)+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{\beta(j)+1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
numbered left to right, so that we can distinguish the copies.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S6.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The copies of the labels satisfying </span><math id="S6.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(j)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(j)=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> become the children
of the root </span><math id="S6.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S6.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We number the nodes in the tree level-by-level and in
increasing order of the labels (breadth-first-search order).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S6.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Once we inserted the copies of all labels </span><math id="S6.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfying </span><math id="S6.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(j)&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(j)&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
all copies of the labels </span><math id="S6.I3.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfying </span><math id="S6.I3.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(j)=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(j)=i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> will be the children
of the node whose number is </span><math id="S6.I3.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.I3.i4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p19.1" class="ltx_p">The parking tree associated to the Athanasiadis-Linusson diagram shown
in Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Example 6.7. ‣ 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is the tree on the left hand side in
Figure <a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. The tree on the right hand side shows the
numbering of the positions in a breadth-first-search order: we call this
the <span id="S6.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">position numbering</span>.
The description of such a parking tree depends on the characterization
of the set of labels which can be siblings. This seems hard in general,
but easy in the following special case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.9</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a sequence <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\beta}=(\beta(1),\beta(2),\ldots,\beta(n))\in\mathbb{N}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\beta}=(\beta(1),\beta(2),\ldots,\beta(n))\in\mathbb{N}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> we define the <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>-extended Shi
arrangement as the hyperplane arrangement</span></p>
<table id="S6.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}=-\beta(j),-\beta(j)+1,\ldots,\beta(j)+1\quad 1\leq i&lt;j\leq n\quad\mbox{in $V_{n-1}$.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">in </mtext><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}=-\beta(j),-\beta(j)+1,\ldots,\beta(j)+1\quad 1\leq i&lt;j\leq n\quad\mbox{in $V_{n-1}$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p20.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, setting <math id="S6.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(1)=\cdots=\beta(n)=a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(1)=\cdots=\beta(n)=a-1</annotation></semantics></math> yields the extended Shi
arrangement <math id="S6.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,a+1}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,a+1}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.10</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The number of regions in a <math id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>-extended Shi
arrangement <math id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(\mathcal{A})=\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n}(\beta(j)+1)+1\right)^{n-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\mathcal{A})=\left(\sum_{j=1}^{n}(\beta(j)+1)+1\right)^{n-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Obviously, the regions of a <math id="S6.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>-extended Shi arrangement have
Athanasiadis-Linusson diagrams. When we build these diagrams,
<math id="S6.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)=\beta(j)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)=\beta(j)+1</annotation></semantics></math> holds exactly when <math id="S6.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, hence we may draw an
arc between the points representing <math id="S6.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}+\beta(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}+\beta(j)</annotation></semantics></math> if and
only if <math id="S6.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}-x_{j}&gt;\beta(j)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}-x_{j}&gt;\beta(j)+1</annotation></semantics></math> hold, and these are all the
potential arcs added in step (3). As a consequence, a set of labels can
be a set of
siblings exactly when for each <math id="S6.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> it contains either all copies of <math id="S6.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>,
or neither of them. We may count all such rooted trees using a colored
variant of the Prüfer code algorithm as follows. We consider the
elements of <math id="S6.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> as colors, and we say that a color <math id="S6.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is
</span><span id="S6.p21.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exposed</span><span id="S6.p21.1.3" class="ltx_text"> if all labels <math id="S6.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are leaves in the tree. In each step
we remove the vertices of the least exposed color and record the common
parent <math id="S6.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(j_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(j_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> in our Prüfer code. We never remove the root
<math id="S6.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>: we consider it the highest numbered vertex. We never remove
<math id="S6.p21.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(j_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(j_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> before all copies of <math id="S6.p21.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as our algorithm removes leaves only
in each step, hence the unique path between any not yet removed vertex and the
root must still be present. For example, for the tree on the
left hand side of Figure <a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the least exposed
color is <math id="S6.p21.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. The vertex <math id="S6.p21.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is also a leaf, but <math id="S6.p21.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is not, hence the
color <math id="S6.p21.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is not yet exposed. There is always at least one exposed
color, because a leaf whose distance from the root is maximum has only
leaf siblings. We stop when
all remaining non-root vertices have the same color. For the tree on the
left hand side of Figure <a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we remove the colors
<math id="S6.p21.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3,4,5,1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3,4,5,1</annotation></semantics></math> in this order and we record the Prüfer code
<math id="S6.p21.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1_{1},0,1_{1},2_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1_{1},0,1_{1},2_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The tree can be uniquely reconstructed from its
colored Prüfer code, as follows. The least nonzero color not present
in the code is <math id="S6.p21.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>: this must be the first removed color, and the common
parent of all vertices of color <math id="S6.p21.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> is the first coordinate of the code,
that is, <math id="S6.p21.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We remove the first
coordinate from the Prüfer code and record <math id="S6.p21.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> as a color in a
separate list of already reinserted vertices: we obtain the pair of
lists <math id="S6.p21.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((0,1_{1},2_{1}),(3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((0,1_{1},2_{1}),(3))</annotation></semantics></math>. The least nonzero color not present in the
current pair of lists is <math id="S6.p21.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the vertices of color <math id="S6.p21.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> have
parent <math id="S6.p21.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, and we get the pair of lists <math id="S6.p21.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((1_{1},2_{1}),(3,4))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((1_{1},2_{1}),(3,4))</annotation></semantics></math>. Now the
least nonzero color not present in our pair of lists is <math id="S6.p21.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> and its
parent is <math id="S6.p21.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We continue the reconstruction of our tree in a similar
fashion. Our colored Prüfer code has <math id="S6.p21.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates and each
coordinate can be either the root or any of the labeled vertices.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>The <math id="S7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan arrangements</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we present two labelings of the regions of the
<math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan arrangement <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>: one
using the Athanasiadis-Linusson diagrams (defined in
Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), the other provides a simple
direct definition of the weighted digraphs using labeled <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan
paths. These labelings are different from each other and also from the
labeling of Duarte and Guedes de Oliveira <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> which
encode the Pak-Stanley labeling using labeled rational Dyck paths. It
seems an interesting question for future research to find direct
bijections between these labelings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The structure of the Athanasiadis-Linusson diagrams is the simplest
possible for the <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan arrangement <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case
<math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(i,j)=a-1=\beta(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(i,j)=a-1=\beta(j)</annotation></semantics></math> holds for all <math id="S7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math> and we may omit step
(3) in constructing the diagrams.</p>
</div>
