<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r(\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1})=an(an-1)\cdots((a-1)n+2)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1})=an(an-1)\cdots((a-1)n+2),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p3.2" class="ltx_p">which is the same as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, Corollary 9.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We may also represent the regions of the <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan arrangement
<math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> using all pairs <math id="S7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi,\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi,\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is a permutation of the set <math id="S7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is an
<math id="S7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-Catalan path</span> with <math id="S7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> up steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan path with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> up steps is a lattice
path containing <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> up steps <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,(a-1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,(a-1))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a-1)n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a-1)n</annotation></semantics></math> down steps <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,-1)</annotation></semantics></math>
from <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(an,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(an,0)</annotation></semantics></math> that stays weakly above the horizontal
axis.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It is well-known that the number of <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan paths with <math id="S7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> up steps is
the <math id="S7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan number
<math id="S7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{(a-1)n+1}\binom{an}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{(a-1)n+1}\binom{an}{n}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, Eq. (7.67)]</cite>.
This representation is easier to visualize but somewhat
mysterious at this time. We use the permutation <math id="S7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> to number the up
steps of <math id="S7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> from
left to right. In the example shown in Figure <a href="#S7.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 7. The 𝑎-Catalan arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> the
permutation <math id="S7.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is simply <math id="S7.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="123456" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>123456</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">123456</annotation></semantics></math>, but we may reuse the same lattice
path with any other permutation. As it was also the case for the
Athanasiadis-Linusson diagrams, the set of valid representations is
invariant under permuting the labels.</p>
</div>
