<div id="S7.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.6</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The correspondence between the pairs <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi,\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi,\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and the valid
weighted <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic digraphs encoded by them is a bijection.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">It suffices to show that the correspondence is injective: by
(<a href="#S7.E1" title="In 7. The 𝑎-Catalan arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>) the number of regions (and of valid <math id="S7.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic
weighted digraphs) is the same as the number of labeled <math id="S7.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan paths.
Consider a labeled <math id="S7.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan path <math id="S7.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi,\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi,\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and the weighted
digraph (described by the weight function <math id="S7.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>) it encodes. By the
results of Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Separated deformations and the weak triangle inequality ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we can compute the total order
of gains <math id="S7.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i)</annotation></semantics></math> and the gain function <math id="S7.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(i)</annotation></semantics></math> from the function
<math id="S7.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 7.4. ‣ 7. The 𝑎-Catalan arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 7.5. ‣ 7. The 𝑎-Catalan arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a> the
gain function is the same as the level function and <math id="S7.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> lists the
up steps level by level, and left to right within the same level. Using
this information we can determine the level of each labeled up step, and
also the relative order of two up steps at the same level. Consider
finally a pair of up steps, labeled with <math id="S7.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively,
whose level is different. If <math id="S7.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\ell(i)-\ell(j)|\leq a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\ell(i)-\ell(j)|\leq a-1</annotation></semantics></math> holds then, by
Equations (<a href="#S7.E2" title="In 7. The 𝑎-Catalan arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>) and  (<a href="#S7.E3" title="In 7. The 𝑎-Catalan arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a>),
<math id="S7.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i,j)=\ell(j)-\ell(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i,j)=\ell(j)-\ell(i)</annotation></semantics></math> holds exactly when the up step labeled <math id="S7.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>
precedes the up step labeled <math id="S7.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. As noted in the proof of
Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 7.5. ‣ 7. The 𝑎-Catalan arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a> the difference of levels between any two
subsequent up steps in the sequence is at most <math id="S7.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-1</annotation></semantics></math>. As a consequence,
we can determine the relative position of any two labeled up steps whose
level is different.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p11.1" class="ltx_p">It is not hard to create the Athanasiadis-Linusson diagram of a region
given by its labeled <math id="S7.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan path <math id="S7.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi,\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi,\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>: the weight
function is easily computed, the linear order of gains <math id="S7.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> can be
read off the labeled lattice path, and a table such as
Table <a href="#S7.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 7. The 𝑎-Catalan arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is easily created. Using the table of the weight
function, we can insert the letters into the Athanasiadis-Linusson word
in the order of <math id="S7.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Finding the inverse of this map appears to be
a much more difficult task.</p>
</div>
