<div id="S7.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude this section with a description of the bounded regions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.7</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A region of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded if and only if the
total order of gains <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\sigma(i),\sigma(i+1))&lt;a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\sigma(i),\sigma(i+1))&lt;a-1</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">By definition we have <math id="S7.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\sigma(i),\sigma(i+1))\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\sigma(i),\sigma(i+1))\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S7.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\sigma(i),\sigma(i+1))&lt;a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\sigma(i),\sigma(i+1))&lt;a-1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, then the directed edges <math id="S7.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i)\rightarrow\sigma(i+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i)\rightarrow\sigma(i+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(i)\leftarrow\sigma(i+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(i)\leftarrow\sigma(i+1)</annotation></semantics></math> are both present, and
the associated weighted digraph is strongly connected. Assume now that
<math id="S7.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\sigma(i_{0}),\sigma(i_{0}+1))=a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\sigma(i_{0}),\sigma(i_{0}+1))=a-1</annotation></semantics></math> holds for some <math id="S7.p13.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1\leq_{0}\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq_{0}\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
In analogy to the case of the extended Shi arrangement the fact that
there is no <math id="S7.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-ascending cycle implies (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In 6. Extended Shi arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>), that is,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w(i_{1},i_{3})\geq\min(a-1),w(i_{1},_{2})+w(i_{2},i_{3}))\quad\mbox{holds for
$i_{1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{2}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{3}$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mtext>holds for </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{1},i_{3})\geq\min(a-1),w(i_{1},_{2})+w(i_{2},i_{3}))\quad\mbox{holds for
$i_{1}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{2}&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{3}$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">Using this inequality it is easy to show that <math id="S7.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(i_{1},i_{2})=a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(i_{1},i_{2})=a-1</annotation></semantics></math> holds for
<math id="S7.p13.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i_{1}\leq_{\sigma^{-1}}i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}\leq_{\sigma^{-1}}i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, there is no
directed edge from the set <math id="S7.p13.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{i_{2}\&gt;i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>i</mi><msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{2}\&gt;i&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i\}</annotation></semantics></math> into the set
<math id="S7.p13.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{i_{1}\&gt;i_{1}\leq&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">&lt;</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></msub><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i_{1}\&gt;i_{1}\leq&lt;_{\sigma^{-1}}i\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The associated weighted digraph
is not strongly connected.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Using Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 7.4. ‣ 7. The 𝑎-Catalan arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>, Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 7.7. ‣ 7. The 𝑎-Catalan arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a> may be
rephrased as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 7.8</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A region of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, encoded by the labeled lattice
path <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi,\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi,\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, is unbounded if and only there are two successive
levels <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell+a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell+a-1</annotation></semantics></math> whose difference is <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-1</annotation></semantics></math> and the last up
step of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> at level <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> precedes the first up step at level
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell+a-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell+a-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p15.1" class="ltx_p">As noted in Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem14" title="Corollary 4.14. ‣ 4. Separated deformations and the weak triangle inequality ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.14</span></a> the number of possible types of
the trees of the gain function is a Catalan number.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 7.9</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a fixed <math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and a fixed tree of gain functions, the number of regions of
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{a,a}_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to it is a polynomial of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Conjecture <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem9" title="Conjecture 7.9. ‣ 7. The 𝑎-Catalan arrangements ‣ Labeling regions in deformations of graphical arrangements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.9</span></a> implies that the
<math id="S7.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th <math id="S7.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>-Catalan number, considered as a polynomial of <math id="S7.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, could be
written as a sum of <math id="S7.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> polynomials, where <math id="S7.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S7.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th Catalan
number.</p>
</div>
</section>
